<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">On-policy distillation (OPD) transfers the behavior of a strong teacher to a student on trajectories sampled by the student itself <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Agarwal et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gu et al., 2024</a>)</cite>. By supervising the states the student actually visits, OPD mitigates the distribution mismatch associated with learning only from fixed teacher demonstrations and provides dense token-level guidance without requiring a task-specific correctness verifier. Although the teacher supplies this supervision, the training questions determine which trajectories the student generates and where teacher feedback becomes available. Constructing effective distillation data therefore requires understanding not only what a question asks, but also what supervision it elicits from a particular student–teacher pair.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S1.F2" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2610.27450v1/ask_to_distill_method_v4.png" id="S1.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/285;" width="476" height="285" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S1.F2.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 2</span>: </span><span id="S1.F2.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Overview of <span id="S1.F2.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">A2D</span>. A student-initialized synthesizer is optimized using learning-value signals from a frozen student–teacher pair. The generated questions are filtered and deduplicated into a prompt-only synthetic bank, which is then used for on-policy distillation with a frozen teacher providing supervision on the student’s top-<math id="S1.F2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> candidates.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Recent studies suggest that OPD can be highly prompt-efficient: small subsets can approach full-data performance, and even a single question can recover much of the gain obtained from a large question bank <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Fu et al., 2026</a>; <a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hou et al., 2026</a>)</cite>. Moreover, self-generated questions can support effective OPD without external seed examples <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2026a</a>)</cite>. These findings motivate a different perspective on OPD training data, centered on the supervision that questions elicit. In supervised fine-tuning, synthetic examples provide target responses for imitation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xu et al., 2024</a>)</cite>; in self-play reinforcement learning, generated tasks provide opportunities to learn from answer-quality or task-success feedback <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Huang et al., 2026</a>; <a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al., 2025</a>)</cite>. OPD instead obtains supervision from the teacher’s token distributions on student-generated prefixes. A question therefore serves as a <em id="S1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">distribution elicitor</em>: it guides the student into states where teacher–student differences become available for learning. From this perspective, training questions act as carriers of distributional knowledge transfer, rather than directly supplying the target knowledge through reference answers. Their distillation value depends on the usable teacher supervision exposed along the induced trajectories for a particular student–teacher pair, consistent with findings that prompt utility is pair-dependent <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2026a</a>)</cite>. Although OPD supplies teacher supervision without reference answers, optimizing a question synthesizer requires an additional signal: feedback on which questions are worth generating. This raises a central question: <em id="S1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">without access to the correct answer to a newly generated question, how can we assess its value for a target student–teacher pair and use that assessment to learn to generate better questions?</em> We address this question by scoring the distributional supervision exposed along student-generated trajectories, using a frozen student–teacher pair without performing a student update for each candidate.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Turning elicited supervision into an optimization objective requires distinguishing disagreement from useful correction. Token-selection and reweighting methods already exploit the unequal value of supervision across response positions <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xu et al., 2026</a>; <a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2026c</a>)</cite>. In particular, support-aware analyses of token teachability show that large divergence can reflect either corrective teacher preferences within the student’s candidate support or a mismatch concentrated outside that support <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2026b</a>)</cite>. Building on this characterization, we combine teacher–student discrepancy with teacher probability mass on the student’s candidate tokens as a surrogate for local learning value. We adapt this token-level principle to question generation by aggregating the signal along student trajectories. This introduces additional challenges: a synthesizer may increase its reward through longer responses or repeatedly produce a narrow family of questions. The question-level objective must therefore balance elicited signal with trajectory length and question diversity.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">We introduce Ask-to-Distill (<span id="S1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">A2D</span>), a self-contained framework that optimizes synthetic questions for a target student–teacher pair before performing OPD. We initialize the question-generation policy from the student and train it with reinforcement learning, while keeping the scoring student and teacher frozen. Its reward aggregates support-aware token signals using sublinear length normalization, a soft response-budget penalty, and a batch-diversity term. We then curate the generated questions through format filtering and lexical and semantic deduplication, and perform standard OPD on the resulting prompt-only dataset. Synthesizer training reuses the target student–teacher pair and requires neither external training questions nor auxiliary models. After training, a frozen Qwen3-Embedding-4B model is used only for semantic deduplication of the generated question bank; it does not participate in synthesizer optimization. Our method operates on the prompt-construction axis, which is complementary to approaches that select supervision positions, reweight trajectories, or modify the downstream distillation procedure.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Our contributions are threefold: (i) we formulate synthetic prompt construction as optimizing the distributional supervision elicited from a target student–teacher pair; (ii) we develop a question-level reward that turns support-aware teacher–student signals into feedback for synthesizer optimization, without reference answers, task-specific correctness rewards, or per-candidate student updates, while controlling response length and question diversity; and (iii) we validate the framework across multiple model scales and mathematical reasoning, code generation, and agentic tasks through downstream comparisons, reward-component ablations, and learning-signal analyses. <span id="S1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">A2D</span> exceeds real-bank OPD on the average scores of the evaluated student–teacher pairs. Our analyses further show that optimized questions can elicit denser distributional signals from shorter student responses, supporting the value of optimizing elicited supervision beyond simply increasing rollout length.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Method</h2>

<section id="S2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.1 </span>Problem formulation: synthetic prompts as distribution elicitors</h3>

<div id="S2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">On-policy distillation (OPD) provides teacher supervision on trajectories sampled by the student. Unlike synthetic examples used for supervised fine-tuning, a question used for OPD need not include a reference response: it supplies the context from which the student generates a trajectory and the teacher provides token-level supervision. Our synthetic data therefore consist of questions only, without ground-truth answers or reference solutions; neither synthesizer reward computation nor downstream OPD requires such annotations. We therefore study synthetic questions as <em id="S2.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">distribution elicitors</em>, whose value depends on the supervision they expose for a particular student–teacher pair.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{S}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{T}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the student and teacher, with policies <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{S}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{T}</annotation></semantics></math>. Given a question <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math>, the student samples a response <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="y\sim\pi_{S}(\cdot\mid q)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y\sim\pi_{S}(\cdot\mid q)</annotation></semantics></math> of length <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math>. At each response position <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>, both models are evaluated on the student-generated prefix <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{t}=(q,y_{&lt;t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{t}=(q,y_{&lt;t})</annotation></semantics></math>, producing next-token distributions <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m10" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\pi_{S}(\cdot\mid c_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{S}(\cdot\mid c_{t})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m11" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\pi_{T}(\cdot\mid c_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{T}(\cdot\mid c_{t})</annotation></semantics></math>. The question thus induces a distribution over the states at which distillation takes place.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Our goal is to construct questions that expose useful teacher supervision along these trajectories. Disagreement alone is insufficient: a question may elicit substantially different predictions while placing little teacher probability mass on the candidates considered by the student’s update. We therefore assess questions using both teacher–student discrepancy and compatibility, while retaining basic format and self-containedness requirements during curation.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">We formalize the elicited signal through a trajectory-based surrogate:</p>
<table id="S2.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathcal{V}(q;\pi_{S},\pi_{T})=\mathbb{E}_{y\sim\pi_{S}(\cdot\mid q)}\left[\frac{1}{L^{\alpha}}\sum_{t=1}^{L}s_{t}\right]," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>;</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi>π</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>S</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub><mrow><mo>[</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msup><mi>L</mi><mi>α</mi></msup></mfrac><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>L</mi></munderover><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{V}(q;\pi_{S},\pi_{T})=\mathbb{E}_{y\sim\pi_{S}(\cdot\mid q)}\left[\frac{1}{L^{\alpha}}\sum_{t=1}^{L}s_{t}\right],</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.p4.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> is a support-aware token score defined in §<a href="#S2.SS3" title="2.3 The learning-value reward ‣ 2 Method ‣ Ask to Distill: Learning to Synthesize Data for On-Policy Distillation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.3</span></a>, and <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> controls the balance between signal density and trajectory length. This quantity measures the distributional signal exposed by a question; it is a surrogate for learning value rather than a direct estimate of downstream performance improvement.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">We parameterize the question distribution with a synthesizer <math id="S2.SS1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math>, initialized as an independent copy of the initial student. During synthesizer optimization, the scoring student and teacher remain frozen, and only <math id="S2.SS1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta</annotation></semantics></math> is updated. The synthesizer’s reward combines a sampled estimate of Eq. (<a href="#S2.E1" title="Equation 1 ‣ 2.1 Problem formulation: synthetic prompts as distribution elicitors ‣ 2 Method ‣ Ask to Distill: Learning to Synthesize Data for On-Policy Distillation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>) with response-length and question-diversity controls. The resulting questions are curated and used for downstream OPD, which starts from the original student parameters. Question generation and synthesizer training use only the target student–teacher pair and the student-initialized synthesizer, without external training questions, labeled examples, or auxiliary models. A separate frozen embedding model is used only for semantic deduplication after question generation (§<a href="#S2.SS5" title="2.5 Synthetic data curation ‣ 2 Method ‣ Ask to Distill: Learning to Synthesize Data for On-Policy Distillation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.5</span></a>).</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.2 </span>Overview</h3>

<div id="S2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The basic pipeline consists of three stages, each performed once. We also consider repeating the pipeline with the updated student in §<a href="#S3.SS10" title="3.10 Iterative Training ‣ 3 Experiments ‣ Ask to Distill: Learning to Synthesize Data for On-Policy Distillation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.10</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<ol id="S2.I1" class="ltx_enumerate" style="--ltx-enum-leftmargin:1.6em;">
<li id="S2.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">1.</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Synthetic question optimization</span> (§<a href="#S2.SS3" title="2.3 The learning-value reward ‣ 2 Method ‣ Ask to Distill: Learning to Synthesize Data for On-Policy Distillation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.3</span></a>–§<a href="#S2.SS4" title="2.4 Optimizing the synthetic data distribution ‣ 2 Method ‣ Ask to Distill: Learning to Synthesize Data for On-Policy Distillation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.4</span></a>). We train <math id="S2.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math> with group relative policy optimization (GRPO) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shao et al., 2024</a>)</cite>. Each generated question is answered by the frozen student, and the frozen teacher is evaluated on the same student-generated prefixes. Their predictions determine a question-level learning-value reward.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">2.</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Synthetic question curation</span> (§<a href="#S2.SS5" title="2.5 Synthetic data curation ‣ 2 Method ‣ Ask to Distill: Learning to Synthesize Data for On-Policy Distillation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.5</span></a>). We sample the trained synthesizer at scale, filter malformed outputs, and remove lexical and semantic duplicates to construct a prompt-only question bank.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">3.</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">On-policy distillation</span> (§<a href="#S2.SS6" title="2.6 On-policy distillation ‣ 2 Method ‣ Ask to Distill: Learning to Synthesize Data for On-Policy Distillation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.6</span></a>). We update the original student using teacher supervision on responses sampled from the curated questions, with the same response budget used for question scoring.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The synthesizer and scoring student share their initial parameters but are separate model copies. Only the synthesizer is updated in stage 1; only the student is updated in stage 3. The teacher remains frozen throughout. Algorithm <a href="#alg1" title="Algorithm 1 ‣ A.1 Algorithm ‣ Appendix A Experimental Details ‣ Ask to Distill: Learning to Synthesize Data for On-Policy Distillation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> in the appendix summarizes the full pipeline; hyperparameter values for all stages are reported in Appendix <a href="#A1.SS2" title="A.2 Implementation Details ‣ Appendix A Experimental Details ‣ Ask to Distill: Learning to Synthesize Data for On-Policy Distillation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.2</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.3 </span>The learning-value reward</h3>

<section id="S2.SS3.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Per-position quantities.</h4>

<div id="S2.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Given a question <math id="S2.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math>, we sample a student response <math id="S2.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="y\sim\pi_{S}(\cdot\mid q)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y\sim\pi_{S}(\cdot\mid q)</annotation></semantics></math> under a response budget <math id="S2.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{\max}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>max</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{\max}</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S2.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L\leq L_{\max}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>max</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L\leq L_{\max}</annotation></semantics></math> be its length and <math id="S2.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{t}=(q,y_{&lt;t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{t}=(q,y_{&lt;t})</annotation></semantics></math> its prefix at position <math id="S2.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>. We write <math id="S2.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{S}(v)=\pi_{S}(v\mid c_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{S}(v)=\pi_{S}(v\mid c_{t})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{T}(v)=\pi_{T}(v\mid c_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{T}(v)=\pi_{T}(v\mid c_{t})</annotation></semantics></math>, suppressing the dependence on <math id="S2.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> for readability. For a candidate size <math id="S2.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math>, we define the student and teacher candidate sets and their union:</p>
<table id="S2.E2" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody>
<tr id="S2.E2X" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E2X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle A_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle A_{t}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E2X.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\mathrm{Top}\text{-}K(p_{S})," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>Top</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>-</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\mathrm{Top}\text{-}K(p_{S}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E2X.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle B_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle B_{t}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E2X.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\mathrm{Top}\text{-}K(p_{T})," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>Top</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>-</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\mathrm{Top}\text{-}K(p_{T}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="2" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equationgroup ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S2.E2Xa" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E2Xa.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle U_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle U_{t}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E2Xa.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=A_{t}\cup B_{t}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∪</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=A_{t}\cup B_{t},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E2Xa.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle|U_{t}|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle|U_{t}|</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E2Xa.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\leq 2K." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\leq 2K.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">For <math id="S2.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M\in\{S,T\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M\in\{S,T\}</annotation></semantics></math>, let</p>
<table id="S2.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{p}_{M}^{U}(v)=\frac{p_{M}(v)}{\sum_{u\in U_{t}}p_{M}(u)},\qquad v\in U_{t}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>p</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>M</mi><mi>U</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>M</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>M</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{p}_{M}^{U}(v)=\frac{p_{M}(v)}{\sum_{u\in U_{t}}p_{M}(u)},\qquad v\in U_{t},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.3" class="ltx_p">denote the corresponding distribution restricted to and renormalized over <math id="S2.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Following support-aware characterizations of token teachability <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2026b</a>)</cite>, we use two quantities to characterize the supervision at each position:</p>
<table id="S2.EGx1" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.E4"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\delta_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\delta_{t}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E4.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle=\mathrm{KL}\!\left(\bar{p}_{T}^{U}\,\|\,\bar{p}_{S}^{U}\right)=\sum_{v\in U_{t}}\bar{p}_{T}^{U}(v)\log\frac{\bar{p}_{T}^{U}(v)}{\bar{p}_{S}^{U}(v)}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>=</mo><mpadded style="width:1.431em;" width="1.431em"><mi>KL</mi></mpadded><mrow><mo>(</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>p</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>T</mi><mi>U</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.337em">∥</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>p</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>S</mi><mi>U</mi></msubsup><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></munder></mstyle><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>p</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>T</mi><mi>U</mi></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>log</mi><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>p</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>T</mi><mi>U</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>p</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>S</mi><mi>U</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\mathrm{KL}\!\left(\bar{p}_{T}^{U}\,\|\,\bar{p}_{S}^{U}\right)=\sum_{v\in U_{t}}\bar{p}_{T}^{U}(v)\log\frac{\bar{p}_{T}^{U}(v)}{\bar{p}_{S}^{U}(v)},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S2.E5"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\omega_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\omega_{t}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\sum_{v\in A_{t}}p_{T}(v)." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></munder></mstyle><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\sum_{v\in A_{t}}p_{T}(v).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(5)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The <em id="S2.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">elicited discrepancy</em> <math id="S2.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> measures differences between teacher and student preferences over their combined candidate set. Its teacher-to-student KL direction emphasizes teacher-preferred candidates to which the student assigns relatively low probability. The <em id="S2.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p3.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">compatibility</em> <math id="S2.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> measures the teacher’s probability mass on the student’s top-<math id="S2.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> candidates, which are the candidates used by our downstream OPD update. Unlike <math id="S2.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> uses the original full-vocabulary teacher probabilities without renormalization.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS3.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Gating discrepancy by compatibility.</h4>

<div id="S2.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Large discrepancy can occur even when the teacher places little probability mass on the student’s candidate set. Candidates outside this set do not receive explicit candidate-level terms in our downstream update (§<a href="#S2.SS6" title="2.6 On-policy distillation ‣ 2 Method ‣ Ask to Distill: Learning to Synthesize Data for On-Policy Distillation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.6</span></a>). We therefore combine discrepancy and compatibility at each position:</p>
<table id="S2.E6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{t}=\min\!\left(\frac{\delta_{t}}{\delta_{\mathrm{ref}}+\epsilon},1\right)\omega_{t}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mpadded style="width:1.653em;" width="1.653em"><mi>min</mi></mpadded><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mrow><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>ref</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{t}=\min\!\left(\frac{\delta_{t}}{\delta_{\mathrm{ref}}+\epsilon},1\right)\omega_{t},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(6)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϵ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math> is a small constant for numerical stability and <math id="S2.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{\mathrm{ref}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>ref</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{\mathrm{ref}}</annotation></semantics></math> is a fixed calibration scale. The resulting score lies in <math id="S2.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math> and favors positions with both substantial discrepancy and teacher probability mass within the student’s candidate support. Multiplying the quantities before aggregation requires them to occur at the same position; multiplying their trajectory-level means could instead combine high discrepancy at one position with high compatibility at another. This construction remains a proxy for useful supervision and does not guarantee that every high-scoring position yields a large learning gain.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS3.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Calibrating <math id="S2.SS3.SSS0.Px3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{\mathrm{ref}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>ref</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{\mathrm{ref}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</h4>

<div id="S2.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We set <math id="S2.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{\mathrm{ref}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>ref</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{\mathrm{ref}}</annotation></semantics></math> to the <math id="S2.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>κ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa</annotation></semantics></math>-th percentile of token-level discrepancies measured on <math id="S2.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{\mathrm{cal}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>cal</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{\mathrm{cal}}</annotation></semantics></math> questions sampled from the initial student policy. The frozen student answers these questions under the response budget used for downstream OPD, and the teacher is evaluated on the resulting student-generated prefixes. We pool the resulting <math id="S2.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> values to compute the percentile and hold it fixed during synthesizer optimization. This calibration uses no external questions and provides a common discrepancy scale across optimization steps.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS3.SSS0.Px4" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Length control.</h4>

<div id="S2.SS3.SSS0.Px4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.SSS0.Px4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We aggregate the token scores using</p>
<table id="S2.E7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{\mathrm{raw}}(q)=\frac{1}{L^{\alpha}}\sum_{t=1}^{L}s_{t}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>raw</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msup><mi>L</mi><mi>α</mi></msup></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>L</mi></munderover><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{\mathrm{raw}}(q)=\frac{1}{L^{\alpha}}\sum_{t=1}^{L}s_{t}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(7)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS3.SSS0.Px4.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Writing <math id="S2.SS3.SSS0.Px4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{s}(q)=L^{-1}\sum_{t=1}^{L}s_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>s</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>L</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>L</mi></msubsup><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{s}(q)=L^{-1}\sum_{t=1}^{L}s_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> gives <math id="S2.SS3.SSS0.Px4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{\mathrm{raw}}(q)=L^{1-\alpha}\bar{s}(q)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>raw</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>L</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>s</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{\mathrm{raw}}(q)=L^{1-\alpha}\bar{s}(q)</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus, <math id="S2.SS3.SSS0.Px4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> controls the balance between average signal and accumulated signal. A per-token mean (<math id="S2.SS3.SSS0.Px4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha=1</annotation></semantics></math>) gives the same score to trajectories with equal average signal regardless of length, while a sum (<math id="S2.SS3.SSS0.Px4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha=0</annotation></semantics></math>) grows linearly with length at fixed average signal. We use an intermediate exponent <math id="S2.SS3.SSS0.Px4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0&lt;\alpha&lt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>α</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0&lt;\alpha&lt;1</annotation></semantics></math> to reward sustained signal while reducing the advantage conferred by additional tokens.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.SSS0.Px4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.SSS0.Px4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">This aggregation retains an incentive for longer responses. We moderate it with a multiplicative penalty based on response-budget consumption:</p>
<table id="S2.E8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{\mathrm{len}}(q)=1-\lambda_{\mathrm{len}}\min\!\left(\frac{L}{L_{\max}},1\right)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>len</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>len</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mpadded style="width:1.653em;" width="1.653em"><mi>min</mi></mpadded><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac><mi>L</mi><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>max</mi></msub></mfrac><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{\mathrm{len}}(q)=1-\lambda_{\mathrm{len}}\min\!\left(\frac{L}{L_{\max}},1\right).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(8)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS3.SSS0.Px4.p2.2" class="ltx_p">Here, <math id="S2.SS3.SSS0.Px4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{\mathrm{len}}\in(0,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>len</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{\mathrm{len}}\in(0,1)</annotation></semantics></math> sets the penalty strength. The penalty decreases with response length and equals <math id="S2.SS3.SSS0.Px4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1-\lambda_{\mathrm{len}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>len</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1-\lambda_{\mathrm{len}}</annotation></semantics></math> at the generation cap. Its multiplicative form makes the relative discount independent of the absolute scale of <math id="S2.SS3.SSS0.Px4.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{\mathrm{raw}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>raw</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{\mathrm{raw}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Together, these controls attenuate length-driven reward growth while retaining a preference for signal accumulated across response positions; they do not eliminate the incentive for longer trajectories.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS3.SSS0.Px5" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Diversity control.</h4>

<div id="S2.SS3.SSS0.Px5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.SSS0.Px5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Optimizing the trajectory-level signal alone can concentrate generation on repetitive questions. Following the cluster-based repetition penalty in R-Zero <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Huang et al., 2026</a>)</cite>, we measure within-batch redundancy using BLEU-based text similarity, without extracting hidden-state representations or using an embedding model.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.SSS0.Px5.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.SSS0.Px5.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S2.SS3.SSS0.Px5.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{B}=\{q_{1},\ldots,q_{N_{\mathcal{B}}}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><msub><mi>N</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{B}=\{q_{1},\ldots,q_{N_{\mathcal{B}}}\}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the full batch of questions scored in one synthesizer-training step. We construct a pairwise distance matrix from one minus the sentence-level BLEU similarity between question texts, following the R-Zero implementation, and apply average-linkage agglomerative clustering with distance threshold <math id="S2.SS3.SSS0.Px5.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math>. If <math id="S2.SS3.SSS0.Px5.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is the cluster label of <math id="S2.SS3.SSS0.Px5.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, its cluster share is</p>
<table id="S2.E9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E9.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathrm{share}(q_{i};\mathcal{B})=\frac{\left|\{j:\ell_{j}=\ell_{i}\}\right|}{N_{\mathcal{B}}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>share</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>;</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo>|</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>j</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>|</mo></mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi></msub></mfrac><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{share}(q_{i};\mathcal{B})=\frac{\left|\{j:\ell_{j}=\ell_{i}\}\right|}{N_{\mathcal{B}}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(9)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS3.SSS0.Px5.p2.2" class="ltx_p">We then define</p>
<table id="S2.E10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{\mathrm{div}}(q_{i};\mathcal{B})=\max\!\left(0,\,1-\lambda_{\mathrm{div}}\mathrm{share}(q_{i};\mathcal{B})\right)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>div</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mpadded style="width:1.808em;" width="1.808em"><mi>max</mi></mpadded><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn> 1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>div</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>share</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{\mathrm{div}}(q_{i};\mathcal{B})=\max\!\left(0,\,1-\lambda_{\mathrm{div}}\mathrm{share}(q_{i};\mathcal{B})\right),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(10)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS3.SSS0.Px5.p2.3" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.SS3.SSS0.Px5.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{\mathrm{div}}&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>div</mi></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{\mathrm{div}}&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> sets the penalty strength.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.SSS0.Px5.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.SSS0.Px5.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Questions in larger text-similarity clusters receive a stronger discount. A singleton cluster receives a factor of <math id="S2.SS3.SSS0.Px5.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1-\lambda_{\mathrm{div}}/N_{\mathcal{B}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>div</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1-\lambda_{\mathrm{div}}/N_{\mathcal{B}}</annotation></semantics></math>, while for <math id="S2.SS3.SSS0.Px5.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{\mathrm{div}}\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>div</mi></msub><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{\mathrm{div}}\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math> a batch assigned entirely to one cluster receives zero reward. The multiplicative form discounts the elicited signal proportionally, avoiding an additive penalty whose relative strength would depend on the signal’s numerical scale. Clustering is performed across the full scoring batch, separately from the within-group advantage normalization used by GRPO.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS3.SSS0.Px6" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Final question-level reward.</h4>

<div id="S2.SS3.SSS0.Px6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.SSS0.Px6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The reward for a question combines its sampled token signals with the length and diversity factors:</p>
<table id="S2.E11" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R(q;\mathcal{B})=\frac{P_{\mathrm{len}}(q)P_{\mathrm{div}}(q;\mathcal{B})}{L^{\alpha}}\sum_{t=1}^{L}s_{t}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>len</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>div</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><msup><mi>L</mi><mi>α</mi></msup></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>L</mi></munderover><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R(q;\mathcal{B})=\frac{P_{\mathrm{len}}(q)P_{\mathrm{div}}(q;\mathcal{B})}{L^{\alpha}}\sum_{t=1}^{L}s_{t}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(11)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS3.SSS0.Px6.p1.2" class="ltx_p">We make the batch dependence explicit because the diversity factor depends on the other questions scored in the same step. The token-signal term is estimated from one student response per question, providing a single-sample estimate of the expectation in Eq. (<a href="#S2.E1" title="Equation 1 ‣ 2.1 Problem formulation: synthetic prompts as distribution elicitors ‣ 2 Method ‣ Ask to Distill: Learning to Synthesize Data for On-Policy Distillation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>) before applying the additional controls. Appendix <a href="#A2" title="Appendix B Theoretical Properties of the Elicited-Signal Objective ‣ Ask to Distill: Learning to Synthesize Data for On-Policy Distillation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B</span></a> establishes a compatible-disagreement coverage bound for the normalized reward and relates compatibility to the OPD candidate advantages.</p>
