<section id="S2.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.4 </span>Optimizing the synthetic data distribution</h3>

<div id="S2.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We initialize <math id="S2.SS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math> from the student to use its existing generative capabilities as the starting point for question construction. Shared initialization supplies a prior for generation; the reward determines which questions are favored for the target student–teacher pair. Throughout this stage, the scoring student and teacher remain frozen.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">For each question-generation instruction <math id="S2.SS4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>, we sample a group of <math id="S2.SS4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> candidate questions from <math id="S2.SS4.p2.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="S_{\theta}(\cdot\mid x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{\theta}(\cdot\mid x)</annotation></semantics></math> under a question budget <math id="S2.SS4.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{q}</annotation></semantics></math>. The synthesis and student-solving prompt templates are provided in Appendix <a href="#A3" title="Appendix C Prompt Templates ‣ Ask to Distill: Learning to Synthesize Data for On-Policy Distillation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C</span></a>. For each candidate <math id="S2.SS4.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, the frozen student generates one response, and the teacher is evaluated on the same response prefixes. These predictions provide the token scores and response-length factor. We compute the batch-diversity factor across the full scoring batch and assign each candidate its reward from Eq. (<a href="#S2.E11" title="Equation 11 ‣ Final question-level reward. ‣ 2.3 The learning-value reward ‣ 2 Method ‣ Ask to Distill: Learning to Synthesize Data for On-Policy Distillation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS4.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS4.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We optimize the synthesizer with GRPO, normalizing rewards within each group of questions sampled from the same instruction. The resulting group-relative advantage is applied to the generated tokens of the corresponding question. Thus, the optimization updates the policy that generates questions; the student responses are used only to score those questions. Operationally, the question-level reward is attached to the final generated question token as a sequence-level outcome reward. This reward closes the feedback loop for question generation: it evaluates a candidate through the supervision exposed by a frozen student–teacher pair and uses that signal to improve the synthesizer. Candidate scoring requires neither reference answers nor task-specific correctness rewards, and does not involve training a student on each candidate. It therefore provides a distributional surrogate for question value before downstream distillation.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS5" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.5 </span>Synthetic data curation</h3>

<div id="S2.SS5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">After synthesizer optimization, we sample <math id="S2.SS5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> raw outputs from the selected checkpoint.
We first filter malformed or incomplete outputs. When a valid question is followed by model self-talk, we retain the question by truncating at the earliest meta-commentary marker. We otherwise reject outputs with repeated <math id="S2.SS5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>-grams, low lexical diversity, unbalanced mathematical delimiters, answer leakage, missing context, or references to absent figures.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS5.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS5.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We then deduplicate the remaining questions in two passes.
Token-set Jaccard matching removes lexical near-duplicates whose similarity reaches a threshold <math id="S2.SS5.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{\mathrm{lex}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>lex</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{\mathrm{lex}}</annotation></semantics></math>. A subsequent greedy semantic pass uses a frozen Qwen3-Embedding-4B model <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2025</a>)</cite>: a question is retained only if the cosine similarity of its embedding to that of every previously retained question is below <math id="S2.SS5.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{\mathrm{sem}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>sem</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{\mathrm{sem}}</annotation></semantics></math>. This embedding model is used only for post-generation deduplication and does not participate in synthesizer training or reward computation.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS5.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS5.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The retained questions form a prompt-only synthetic bank. Any placeholder answers stored for file-format compatibility are ignored during OPD; all training responses are sampled from the student.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS6" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.6 </span>On-policy distillation</h3>

<div id="S2.SS6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We use the curated question bank for candidate-restricted OPD, starting from the original student parameters. For each question, the current rollout policy <math id="S2.SS6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{S,\mathrm{old}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>old</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{S,\mathrm{old}}</annotation></semantics></math> samples <math id="S2.SS6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{\mathrm{roll}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>roll</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{\mathrm{roll}}</annotation></semantics></math> responses under the response budget <math id="S2.SS6.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{\max}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>max</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{\max}</annotation></semantics></math> used for question scoring. The teacher remains frozen and is evaluated on the student-generated prefixes.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS6.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS6.p2.1" class="ltx_p">At each response position <math id="S2.SS6.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>, let <math id="S2.SS6.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{S,\mathrm{old}}(v)=\pi_{S,\mathrm{old}}(v\mid c_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>old</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>old</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{S,\mathrm{old}}(v)=\pi_{S,\mathrm{old}}(v\mid c_{t})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS6.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{T}(v)=\pi_{T}(v\mid c_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{T}(v)=\pi_{T}(v\mid c_{t})</annotation></semantics></math>. We construct <math id="S2.SS6.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{t}=\mathrm{Top}\text{-}K(p_{S,\mathrm{old}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>Top</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>-</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>old</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{t}=\mathrm{Top}\text{-}K(p_{S,\mathrm{old}})</annotation></semantics></math> with the same <math id="S2.SS6.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> as in §<a href="#S2.SS3" title="2.3 The learning-value reward ‣ 2 Method ‣ Ask to Distill: Learning to Synthesize Data for On-Policy Distillation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.3</span></a> and define the student probabilities normalized over this set:</p>
<table id="S2.E12" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{p}_{S,\mathrm{old}}^{A}(v)=\frac{p_{S,\mathrm{old}}(v)}{\sum_{u\in A_{t}}p_{S,\mathrm{old}}(u)},\qquad v\in A_{t}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>p</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>old</mi></mrow><mi>A</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>old</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>old</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{p}_{S,\mathrm{old}}^{A}(v)=\frac{p_{S,\mathrm{old}}(v)}{\sum_{u\in A_{t}}p_{S,\mathrm{old}}(u)},\qquad v\in A_{t}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(12)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS6.p2.2" class="ltx_p">The superscript <math id="S2.SS6.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> distinguishes this normalization from the union-support normalization used to score questions in §<a href="#S2.SS3" title="2.3 The learning-value reward ‣ 2 Method ‣ Ask to Distill: Learning to Synthesize Data for On-Policy Distillation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.3</span></a>.</p>
</div>
