<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS6.p5.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.SS6.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> masks valid response positions and the expectation is over sampled questions and student responses. Gradients flow through <math id="S2.SS6.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{S,\phi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ϕ</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{S,\phi}</annotation></semantics></math>, while the rollout probabilities, candidate sets, and advantages are treated as constants.
We use a single inner epoch per rollout batch, without a value function, return accumulation, group normalization of these advantages, or a KL anchor to a reference model.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS6.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS6.p6.1" class="ltx_p">At the beginning of an update, the trainable student matches the rollout policy and <math id="S2.SS6.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{t}(v)=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{t}(v)=1</annotation></semantics></math>; the ratios can change as optimization proceeds. The advantages provide a student-probability-weighted, reverse-KL-style learning signal restricted to the student’s top-<math id="S2.SS6.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> candidates. We do not identify this clipped, candidate-restricted objective with exact full-vocabulary reverse-KL minimization.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS6.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS6.p7.1" class="ltx_p">The question-generation reward and the downstream update serve different roles. The forward-KL discrepancy in Eq.(<a href="#S2.E4" title="Equation 4 ‣ Per-position quantities. ‣ 2.3 The learning-value reward ‣ 2 Method ‣ Ask to Distill: Learning to Synthesize Data for On-Policy Distillation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>) identifies teacher-preferred candidates that the scoring student underweights, while compatibility measures how much teacher probability mass lies within the candidate set used for training. Their combination scores the supervision a question exposes without reproducing the downstream loss or directly estimating its gradient magnitude. We hold the downstream OPD procedure fixed across prompt-source comparisons to assess the effect of the constructed question distribution.</p>
</div>
