<div id="S3.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">(5) <span id="S3.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Evol-Instruct</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xu et al., 2024</a>)</cite>: an instruction-evolution method that iteratively rewrites seed instructions to increase complexity and diversity, which we adapt to construct mathematical and programming questions for OPD;
(6) <span id="S3.SS2.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">R-Zero</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Huang et al., 2026</a>)</cite>: a self-evolution framework in which a Challenger proposes tasks and a Solver learns to solve them, with Challenger optimization guided by feedback about the Solver’s capabilities, whose task-generation procedure we use to construct mathematical questions for OPD;
(7) <span id="S3.SS2.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">SQLM</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al., 2025</a>)</cite>: an asymmetric self-play framework with a topic-conditioned proposer and a solver, whose coding formulation uses generated unit tests and solver test performance to encourage non-trivial but solvable tasks, providing programming questions for downstream OPD. Evol-Instruct is evaluated in both domains, while R-Zero and SQLM are included in the mathematical and code-generation comparisons, respectively. For these question-bank comparisons, the generated or selected questions are used as prompts for the same downstream OPD procedure. We reproduce Evol-Instruct, R-Zero, and SQLM using their official implementations.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.3 </span>Datasets and Evaluation Metrics</h3>

<section id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Mathematical reasoning.</h4>

<div id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We evaluate on six benchmarks: AIME24 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mathematical Association of America, 2024</a>)</cite>, AIME25 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang and Math-AI, 2025</a>)</cite>, AIME26 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dekoninck et al., 2026</a>)</cite>, AMC23 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mathematical Association of America, 2023</a>)</cite>, BRUMO25 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dekoninck et al., 2026</a>)</cite>, and MATH500 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hendrycks et al., 2021b</a>; <a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lightman et al., 2024</a>)</cite>. Each AIME benchmark and BRUMO25 contains 30 problems, AMC23 contains 40, and MATH500 contains 500. These benchmarks cover competition-level mathematical reasoning across algebra, geometry, number theory, and combinatorics. We evaluate final-answer correctness using rule-based normalization and symbolic equivalence checking.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Code generation.</h4>

<div id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We evaluate on six benchmarks: LiveCodeBench <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jain et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, MBPP+ <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">Austin et al., 2021</a>)</cite>, the introductory, interview, and competition subsets of APPS <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hendrycks et al., 2021a</a>)</cite>, and CodeContests <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2022</a>)</cite>. We use the LiveCodeBench v5–v6 subset with 342 problems and MBPP+ v0.2.0 with 378 problems. For APPS, we use all 1,000 introductory and 1,000 competition test problems, together with a fixed sample of 1,000 interview test problems. CodeContests contains 165 test problems. A generated program is considered correct only if it passes every test case used by the evaluator within the time limit. For MBPP+, we apply entry-point name normalization when the required function is absent and the generated program defines exactly one top-level function.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Agentic tasks.</h4>

<div id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We additionally evaluate on the Airline, Retail, and Telecom domains of <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>τ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>-Bench <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">Barrès et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, using the official test splits containing 20, 40, and 40 tasks, respectively. These tasks require an agent to interact with a simulated user and invoke tools to complete domain-specific objectives. We run four trials per task and report <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{pass}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>pass</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{pass}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, the average single-trial task success rate under the benchmark evaluator.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px4" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Evaluation metrics.</h4>

<div id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Following <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al. (2026b)</a></cite>, for mathematical reasoning and code generation, we report <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{avg}@k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>avg</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>@</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{avg}@k</annotation></semantics></math>, defined as the fraction of correct responses among <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> generations per problem, averaged over all problems:</p>
<table id="S3.E16" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{avg}@k=\frac{100}{Nk}\sum_{i=1}^{N}\sum_{j=1}^{k}\mathbb{I}\!\left[y_{i}^{(j)}\text{ is correct}\right]," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>avg</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>@</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>100</mn><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></munderover><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></munderover><mrow><mpadded style="width:0.219em;" width="0.219em"><mi>𝕀</mi></mpadded><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext> is correct</mtext></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{avg}@k=\frac{100}{Nk}\sum_{i=1}^{N}\sum_{j=1}^{k}\mathbb{I}\!\left[y_{i}^{(j)}\text{ is correct}\right],</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(16)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px4.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> is the number of problems. This metric measures average response accuracy rather than whether at least one of the <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> responses is correct. We use <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=16" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>16</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=16</annotation></semantics></math> for AIME24/25/26, AMC23, and BRUMO25; <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=4</annotation></semantics></math> for MATH500 and LiveCodeBench; and <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px4.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=1</annotation></semantics></math> for the remaining code benchmarks. MBPP+ uses greedy decoding. Within each domain, <span id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px4.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Avg.</span> is the unweighted mean of the benchmark scores. To measure how much of the initial student–teacher performance gap is recovered, we also report</p>
<table id="S3.E17" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Recovery}=100\times\frac{\mathrm{Avg.}_{\mathrm{method}}-\mathrm{Avg.}_{\mathrm{student}}}{\mathrm{Avg.}_{\mathrm{teacher}}-\mathrm{Avg.}_{\mathrm{student}}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>Recovery</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>100</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>Avg</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">.</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>method</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi>Avg</mi><mo lspace="0em">.</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>student</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><mi>Avg</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">.</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>teacher</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi>Avg</mi><mo lspace="0em">.</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>student</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Recovery}=100\times\frac{\mathrm{Avg.}_{\mathrm{method}}-\mathrm{Avg.}_{\mathrm{student}}}{\mathrm{Avg.}_{\mathrm{teacher}}-\mathrm{Avg.}_{\mathrm{student}}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(17)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px4.p1.3" class="ltx_p">A recovery of <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px4.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="100\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>100</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">100\%</annotation></semantics></math> indicates that the distilled student matches the teacher’s average performance, while values above <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px4.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="100\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>100</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">100\%</annotation></semantics></math> indicate that it exceeds the teacher. Recovery is computed from unrounded averages. Detailed decoding settings and evaluation procedures are provided in Appendix <a href="#A1.SS3" title="A.3 Evaluation Details ‣ Appendix A Experimental Details ‣ Ask to Distill: Learning to Synthesize Data for On-Policy Distillation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.3</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F5" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2610.27450v1/token_efficiency_gain_loss.svg" id="S3.F5.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_portrait" style="aspect-ratio:261/342;" width="261" height="342"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S3.F5.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 5</span>: </span><span id="S3.F5.6" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Average accuracy versus cumulative training tokens for (a)
