</figure>
<figure id="S3.F6" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2610.27450v1/case_study.png" id="S3.F6.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/328;" width="476" height="328" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S3.F6.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 6</span>: </span><span id="S3.F6.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">Simple prompts can elicit dense distillation signals.<span id="S3.F6.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> Comparison of (a) a real question from DAPO-17k-Math and (b) a synthetic question generated by our method. Darker token backgrounds indicate higher support-aware scores <math id="S3.F6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>; the strips summarize the complete rollouts, with boxes marking the displayed excerpts. Although both student answers are incorrect, the apparently simpler synthetic question elicits a higher mean <math id="S3.F6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> (0.172 versus 0.117; approximately <math id="S3.F6.m6" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="1.5\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1.5</mn><mo lspace="0.222em">×</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1.5\times</annotation></semantics></math>) with a shorter rollout (1,241 versus 2,002 tokens), illustrating that surface complexity alone does not characterize the density of elicited supervision.</span></span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">All reported results are means over three independent runs with different random seeds. Evaluation settings, including decoding parameters and answer verification, are described in Appendix <a href="#A1.SS3" title="A.3 Evaluation Details ‣ Appendix A Experimental Details ‣ Ask to Distill: Learning to Synthesize Data for On-Policy Distillation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.3</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.4 </span>Main Results</h3>

<div id="S3.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We evaluate <span id="S3.SS4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">A2D</span> across six mathematical reasoning and six code-generation benchmarks, with three student–teacher pairs in each domain. Tables <a href="#S2.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ Final question-level reward. ‣ 2.3 The learning-value reward ‣ 2 Method ‣ Ask to Distill: Learning to Synthesize Data for On-Policy Distillation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> and <a href="#S2.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ 2.6 On-policy distillation ‣ 2 Method ‣ Ask to Distill: Learning to Synthesize Data for On-Policy Distillation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> report the main results.
Across all six settings, <span id="S3.SS4.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">A2D</span> achieves the highest average performance among the compared distillation methods, demonstrating the effectiveness of optimizing synthetic questions for the target student–teacher pair.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS4.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Mathematical reasoning.</h4>

<div id="S3.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">As shown in Table <a href="#S2.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ Final question-level reward. ‣ 2.3 The learning-value reward ‣ 2 Method ‣ Ask to Distill: Learning to Synthesize Data for On-Policy Distillation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, <span id="S3.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">A2D</span> achieves average scores of 46.2, 51.6, and 64.5 for Qwen3-1.7B, R1-Distill-1.5B, and R1-Distill-7B, respectively, compared with 43.0, 50.5, and 62.9 for Vanilla OPD. These results show that optimized synthetic questions can outperform the original real question bank across both non-thinking and reasoning students. The corresponding teacher-gap recovery rates reach 90.2%, 99.9%, and 81.3%; notably, R1-Distill-1.5B nearly matches its teacher’s average performance. Relative to Student-Generated OPD, our method improves the reported average scores by 5.1, 3.4, and 3.7 percentage points, indicating that optimizing the synthesizer provides additional value beyond sampling questions directly from the student.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">A2D</span> also outperforms KL-Filtered OPD, Evol-Instruct, and R-Zero on the average score in every mathematical setting. The comparison with KL-Filtered OPD supports the value of optimizing the prompt distribution using support-aware supervision beyond selecting real questions with large teacher–student disagreement. At the benchmark level, representative gains over Vanilla OPD include an increase from 20.0 to 26.5 on AIME26 for Qwen3-1.7B and from 78.1 to 83.6 on AMC23 for R1-Distill-1.5B. The improvements are not uniform across benchmarks: other methods remain stronger on some BRUMO25 and MATH500 evaluations. Nevertheless, the consistent average gains indicate that our optimized question banks provide effective supervision across the evaluated mathematical tasks.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS4.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Code generation.</h4>

<div id="S3.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Table <a href="#S2.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ 2.6 On-policy distillation ‣ 2 Method ‣ Ask to Distill: Learning to Synthesize Data for On-Policy Distillation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> shows that <span id="S3.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">A2D</span> also improves code-generation performance across all three student–teacher pairs.
Its average scores reach 39.6, 35.7, and 21.2, compared with 34.5, 30.2, and 20.2 for Vanilla OPD, respectively. Our method also exceeds Student-Generated OPD, KL-Filtered OPD, Evol-Instruct, and SQLM in average performance, and achieves the best or tied-best score among the compared distillation methods in 16 of the 18 benchmark–pair combinations. The gains extend across multiple coding benchmarks. For example, with Qwen3-30B-A3B as the teacher, APPS-Interview improves from 21.3 to 31.6 and CodeContests from 4.8 to 13.9 relative to Vanilla OPD. These results extend the benefits of optimizing elicited supervision beyond mathematical reasoning to code generation.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The magnitude of improvement nevertheless varies substantially across student–teacher pairs. Teacher-gap recovery reaches 82.9% for Qwen3-4B distilled from the 4B teacher, but remains at 32.8% with the stronger 30B-A3B teacher and 12.3% for the Qwen2.5-Coder-3B/32B pair. Moreover, for the same Qwen3-4B student, the stronger teacher yields a lower distilled average score than the 4B teacher under our setup. This comparison indicates that teacher benchmark performance alone does not determine downstream distillation effectiveness. Optimizing the question distribution consistently improves the evaluated outcomes, while substantial headroom remains in some student–teacher settings.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS5" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.5 </span>Ablation: Can a stronger model replace synthetic data optimization?</h3>

<div id="S3.SS5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A natural alternative to training a synthesizer for each student–teacher pair is
to ask a stronger off-the-shelf model—in our setting, the teacher itself—to
generate the questions. To separate the effect of synthesizer initialization from
that of pair-specific optimization, we compare student- and teacher-initialized
synthesizers, each with and without RL, while holding the downstream distillation
setup fixed. Figure <a href="#S2.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 2.6 On-policy distillation ‣ 2 Method ‣ Ask to Distill: Learning to Synthesize Data for On-Policy Distillation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> reports performance over training
for a representative code student–teacher pair; the dashed lines mark the
student and teacher reference scores.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS5.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS5.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Without RL, teacher initialization improves over student initialization on the
two APPS subsets, but not on LCB, and neither unoptimized synthesizer matches the
student-initialized RL variant across the three benchmarks. Applying RL improves
the trajectories for both initializations, indicating that the benefit is not
explained by simply prompting a stronger model to write questions. The
student-initialized RL variant reaches roughly 35% on LCB, above the teacher
reference of 33.7%, and about 62% on APPS-Intro. On APPS-Interview it reaches
about 36%; both APPS scores remain below their teacher references (65.6% and
41.0%, respectively). The teacher-initialized RL variant is competitive—and
slightly higher at some APPS-Intro checkpoints—but does not consistently
dominate the student-initialized variant. Thus, synthesizer strength alone is not
a substitute for optimizing questions against the target pair, while the choice
of initialization can still affect performance by benchmark. These curves are
descriptive: they show checkpoint trends, not uncertainty across independent
runs.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS6" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.6 </span>Ablation: which reward components matter?</h3>

<div id="S3.SS6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We ablate compatibility, the training-time diversity term, and the explicit
length penalty, and evaluate the resulting question banks by downstream best
accuracy on AIME24, AIME25, and AIME26 (Figure <a href="#S3.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ 3.2 Baselines ‣ 3 Experiments ‣ Ask to Distill: Learning to Synthesize Data for On-Policy Distillation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>). The
full reward performs best on all three benchmarks, reaching 45.0%, 35.8%, and
29.2%, respectively. Removing any one of the three components lowers accuracy
on every benchmark, with drops ranging from 2.1 to 6.6 percentage points.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS6.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS6.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The size of each drop depends on the benchmark. On AIME25, removing
compatibility, diversity, or the length penalty reduces accuracy by 6.2, 6.0,
and 6.6 points, respectively. On AIME24, the largest reduction is 4.2 points
without diversity; on AIME26, it is 3.4 points without the length penalty. These
results suggest that all three components contribute to the performance of the
complete reward, while their relative importance varies across benchmarks. The
figure reports downstream accuracy rather than synthesizer reward, directly
assessing the quality of the prompts for distillation.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS7" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.7 </span>Applicability to agentic tasks</h3>

<div id="S3.SS7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS7.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To test whether <span id="S3.SS7.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">A2D</span> applies beyond mathematical reasoning and code
generation, we evaluate it on the three agentic task domains in <math id="S3.SS7.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>τ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>-Bench:
Airline, Retail, and Telecom. Table <a href="#S2.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ 2.6 On-policy distillation ‣ 2 Method ‣ Ask to Distill: Learning to Synthesize Data for On-Policy Distillation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> reports results for
Qwen3-4B (non-thinking).</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS7.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS7.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Our method reaches 46.3%, 49.4%, and 30.0% in the three domains,
respectively, for an average of 41.9%. This exceeds Student-Generated OPD by
3.8, 6.9, and 3.1 percentage points, respectively (4.6 points on average), and
raises teacher-gap recovery from 79.2% to 95.2%. It also outperforms Vanilla
OPD (40.4% average) and KL-Filtered OPD (40.9%) on the average score. For this
student–teacher pair, these results support the applicability of pair-specific
question optimization to agentic task domains as well as to reasoning and code
generation. The average remains 1.4 percentage points below the teacher
reference (43.3%).</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS8" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.8 </span>Cross-Domain Transfer between Math and Code</h3>

<div id="S3.SS8.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS8.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our distribution-elicitation perspective suggests that the benefits of OPD need not be confined to the domain of its training questions. We examine this hypothesis in two directions: code-to-math transfer with Qwen3-4B (non-thinking) distilled from Qwen3-4B-Instruct-2507, and math-to-code transfer with R1-Distill-7B distilled from Skywork-OR1-Math-7B. In each setting, we directly evaluate the student after source-domain OPD, without additional target-domain training, comparing our optimized synthetic questions with the real source-domain questions used by Vanilla OPD. We report gains over the initial student and compute an unweighted average across the target benchmarks.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS8.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Code-to-math transfer.</h4>

<div id="S3.SS8.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS8.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We evaluate code-trained students on AIME24, AIME26, AMC23, and BRUMO25. As shown in Figure <a href="#S3.F7" title="Figure 7 ‣ Math-to-code transfer. ‣ 3.8 Cross-Domain Transfer between Math and Code ‣ 3 Experiments ‣ Ask to Distill: Learning to Synthesize Data for On-Policy Distillation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>(a), both methods improve performance on all four mathematics benchmarks. <span id="S3.SS8.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">A2D</span> raises average accuracy from 31.38% to 38.60%, a gain of 7.23 percentage points, compared with 5.33 points for Vanilla OPD. Our method consistently yields larger improvements across the four benchmarks, with gains of 11.0, 5.2, 8.5, and 4.2 points, respectively. These results show that supervision elicited by code questions can also benefit mathematical reasoning.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS8.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Math-to-code transfer.</h4>

<div id="S3.SS8.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS8.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We evaluate mathematics-trained students on LiveCodeBench, APPS-Intro, and APPS-Interview. Figure <a href="#S3.F7" title="Figure 7 ‣ Math-to-code transfer. ‣ 3.8 Cross-Domain Transfer between Math and Code ‣ 3 Experiments ‣ Ask to Distill: Learning to Synthesize Data for On-Policy Distillation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>(b) shows that <span id="S3.SS8.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">A2D</span> improves performance by 2.7, 4.8, and 8.7 percentage points, respectively, compared with 1.9, 3.8, and 6.7 points for Vanilla OPD. Average accuracy increases from 47.00% to 52.40% with our method, exceeding Vanilla OPD’s 51.13% and approaching the teacher’s 52.70%. The student also surpasses the teacher on APPS-Intro (70.1% versus 68.7%), while remaining below it on the other two benchmarks.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS8.SSS0.Px2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS8.SSS0.Px2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Together, these results demonstrate cross-domain gains in both evaluated directions, with our optimized synthetic questions outperforming real source-domain questions on every reported target benchmark. This finding is consistent with questions serving as carriers of distributional supervision whose benefits extend beyond the training domain. Although these experiments do not isolate the mechanism underlying transfer, they show that optimizing the supervision elicited from a particular student–teacher pair can improve performance beyond source-domain tasks.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F7" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2610.27450v1/cross_domain_transfer_2.svg" id="S3.F7.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/164;" width="476" height="164"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S3.F7.6" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 7</span>: </span><span id="S3.F7.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">Cross-domain transfer between math and code.<span id="S3.F7.7.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> Gains over the initial student after source-domain OPD, without additional target-domain training. (a) Code-to-math transfer with Qwen3-4B (non-thinking) distilled from Qwen3-4B-Instruct-2507. (b) Math-to-code transfer with R1-Distill-7B distilled from Skywork-OR1-Math-7B. <span id="S3.F7.7.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">A2D</span> uses optimized synthetic source-domain questions, while Vanilla OPD uses real source-domain questions. Bars report gains in percentage points; shaded groups show unweighted averages across the displayed benchmarks. Both methods improve all seven evaluated pair–benchmark combinations, and <span id="S3.F7.7.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">A2D</span> consistently yields larger gains.</span></span></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS9" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.9 </span>Training-token efficiency and checkpoint gains</h3>

<div id="S3.SS9.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS9.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We compare downstream accuracy against cumulative training tokens for
R1-Distill-1.5B on JustRL and R1-Distill-7B on Skywork
(Figure <a href="#S3.F5" title="Figure 5 ‣ Evaluation metrics. ‣ 3.3 Datasets and Evaluation Metrics ‣ 3 Experiments ‣ Ask to Distill: Learning to Synthesize Data for On-Policy Distillation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>). The plot shows the Vanilla and <span id="S3.SS9.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">A2D</span> checkpoint trajectories, the Vanilla best-so-far reference, and the best
checkpoint for each method. <span id="S3.SS9.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">A2D</span> reaches a best accuracy of 54.80% on
R1-Distill-1.5B, compared with 52.27% for Vanilla, a gain of 2.53 percentage
points. On R1-Distill-7B, <span id="S3.SS9.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">A2D</span> reaches 63.80% versus 61.70% for Vanilla, a
gain of 2.10 points. The best-checkpoint token counts are similar for the
1.5B model, while <span id="S3.SS9.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">A2D</span> reaches its best checkpoint with substantially fewer
training tokens for the 7B model.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS9.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS9.p2.1" class="ltx_p">These trajectories indicate that the accuracy gains are achieved without a
substantial increase in downstream training-token use, particularly for
R1-Distill-7B. They report downstream accuracy and token use only; they do not
establish lower end-to-end cost, which also depends on the upstream cost of
generating and scoring synthetic questions.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS10" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.10 </span>Iterative Training</h3>

<div id="S3.SS10.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS10.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our main framework optimizes synthetic questions for a fixed student–teacher pair before performing OPD. We further examine whether repeating this process as the student improves yields additional benefits. We conduct three rounds on the code-generation pair consisting of a Qwen3-4B student and a Qwen3-30B-A3B teacher. After each round, we update the student used for question optimization, construct a new synthetic question bank, and continue OPD while keeping the teacher fixed. We evaluate on LiveCodeBench, APPS-Intro, and APPS-Interview and report the highest observed average accuracy across the evaluated checkpoints within each round. This comparison provides an optimistic assessment of the benefits of additional rounds, rather than a validation-selected final result.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS10.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS10.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The first round raises average accuracy from 29.03% to 40.53%, an improvement of 11.50 percentage points. In contrast, the best observed averages in the second and third rounds reach only 40.82% and 40.97%, respectively. Thus, two additional rounds yield a cumulative gain of just 0.44 points over the first round, despite the additional question-generation and distillation effort. Even under this best-checkpoint comparison, iterative training provides only marginal improvements in the evaluated setting.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS10.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS10.p3.1" class="ltx_p">One possible explanation is that the first round already captures much of the readily accessible supervision for this student–teacher pair. As the student becomes better aligned with the teacher, the synthesizer may find it increasingly difficult to identify questions that expose substantial disagreement while remaining within regions where the teacher provides useful guidance. In terms of our learning-value objective, maintaining both disagreement and compatibility may therefore become harder across rounds. This interpretation remains a hypothesis, as downstream scores alone do not establish how these signals evolve during training. Nevertheless, the observed diminishing returns suggest that repeatedly adapting the question distribution is not sufficient to sustain the large gains achieved in the first round, supporting single-round optimization as a practical choice in this setting.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS11" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.11 </span>Case study: where does the teacher provide useful signal?</h3>

<div id="S3.SS11.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS11.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To examine what the learning-value reward captures at the token level,
Figure <a href="#S3.F6" title="Figure 6 ‣ Evaluation metrics. ‣ 3.3 Datasets and Evaluation Metrics ‣ 3 Experiments ‣ Ask to Distill: Learning to Synthesize Data for On-Policy Distillation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a> contrasts a real geometry question from DAPO-17k-Math
with a short combinatorics question posed by our student-initialized
synthesizer, both scored with the same student–teacher pair. Each panel reports
the apparent difficulty of the question, the mean teacher–student discrepancy,
and the mean support-aware score <math id="S3.SS11.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> (§<a href="#S2.SS3" title="2.3 The learning-value reward ‣ 2 Method ‣ Ask to Distill: Learning to Synthesize Data for On-Policy Distillation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.3</span></a>) over one student
rollout. Below these statistics, an excerpt of the rollout is shaded by <math id="S3.SS11.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>
and discrepancy, and two strips summarize both quantities over the full
response, with a box marking the excerpt. The student answers both questions
incorrectly.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS11.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Signal density.</h4>

<div id="S3.SS11.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS11.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Despite its simpler surface form, the synthetic question elicits denser
supervision. Its rollout contains 1,241 tokens, compared with 2,002 for the
real question, while achieving approximately <math id="S3.SS11.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="1.5\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1.5</mn><mo lspace="0.222em">×</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1.5\times</annotation></semantics></math> the mean <math id="S3.SS11.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>
(0.172 versus 0.117) and <math id="S3.SS11.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="1.7\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1.7</mn><mo lspace="0.222em">×</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1.7\times</annotation></semantics></math> the mean discrepancy (0.513 versus 0.307).
Thus, the synthetic question produces a 38% shorter trajectory with a higher
average support-aware score. Its cumulative score remains comparable to that of the real question
(<math id="S3.SS11.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{t}s_{t}=214" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mi>t</mi></msub><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>214</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{t}s_{t}=214</annotation></semantics></math> versus <math id="S3.SS11.SSS0.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="234" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>234</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">234</annotation></semantics></math>), retaining approximately 91% of the scored
signal with only 62% of the response tokens.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS11.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Where the signal arises.</h4>

<div id="S3.SS11.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS11.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The excerpts indicate where this difference comes from. In the geometry
rollout, the displayed segment consists of routine coordinate algebra: the
teacher largely agrees with the student token by token, and most positions
receive low scores. In the combinatorics rollout, high-scoring positions
cluster at the decisions that structure the solution—treating B and S as a
single entity and then counting the resulting items—including the step
where the student counts three items although there are four (U, E, the
B–S block, and C). In both rollouts, <math id="S3.SS11.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> is almost perfectly
rank-correlated with the discrepancy (Spearman <math id="S3.SS11.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho=0.994" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.994</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho=0.994</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS11.SSS0.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.997" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.997</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.997</annotation></semantics></math>).
Thus, the combined score largely preserves the ordering of positions by
discrepancy in these examples. This rank correlation alone does not determine
the magnitude of the compatibility factor.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F8" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2610.27450v1/signal_efficiency.svg" id="S3.F8.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:215/215;" width="215" height="215"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S3.F8.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 8</span>: </span><span id="S3.F8.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">Optimized questions elicit more signal with fewer tokens.<span id="S3.F8.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> We compare student rollouts on 1,000 questions each from DAPO-17k-Math and our synthetic bank. (a) Mean response length and cumulative support-aware score <math id="S3.F8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{t}s_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mi>t</mi></msub><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{t}s_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>, normalized to the real-question baseline (100%); labels above bars show raw means. Synthetic questions yield 20% shorter responses and 28% more cumulative signal. (b) Mean token-level score <math id="S3.F8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> across relative response-position bins. Synthetic questions maintain higher scores throughout the response, indicating that the signal-density advantage extends across the trajectory.</span></span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S3.SS11.SSS0.Px2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS11.SSS0.Px2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">This example illustrates that surface complexity alone is an incomplete
indicator of a question’s value for OPD: a concise, apparently straightforward
problem can elicit dense teacher–student disagreement with substantial
support-aware signal. The observed advantage concerns signal density; the
cumulative support-aware score is slightly lower for the synthetic question.
These observations provide a qualitative illustration of our
distribution-elicitation perspective, complementing the downstream performance
comparisons.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS11.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Aggregate signal analysis.</h4>

<div id="S3.SS11.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS11.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To complement the individual examples, we sample 1,000 questions each from DAPO-17k-Math and our synthetic question bank and analyze the support-aware signal along student rollouts. As shown in Figure <a href="#S3.F8" title="Figure 8 ‣ Where the signal arises. ‣ 3.11 Case study: where does the teacher provide useful signal? ‣ 3 Experiments ‣ Ask to Distill: Learning to Synthesize Data for On-Policy Distillation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>(a), synthetic questions elicit shorter responses on average (1,020.7 versus 1,280.2 tokens, a 20% reduction), yet yield a higher mean cumulative score <math id="S3.SS11.SSS0.Px3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{t}s_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mi>t</mi></msub><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{t}s_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> (181.7 versus 141.7, a 28% increase). Thus, across these samples, the higher signal density translates into more total scored signal despite fewer response tokens. Figure <a href="#S3.F8" title="Figure 8 ‣ Where the signal arises. ‣ 3.11 Case study: where does the teacher provide useful signal? ‣ 3 Experiments ‣ Ask to Distill: Learning to Synthesize Data for On-Policy Distillation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>(b) further shows that the mean <math id="S3.SS11.SSS0.Px3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> remains higher for synthetic questions across all displayed relative-position bins, indicating that the advantage extends throughout the response rather than being confined to a narrow segment. Together with the concrete examples, these aggregate results support our distribution-elicitation perspective: optimized questions can expose denser and more abundant support-aware supervision without relying on longer student trajectories.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS12" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.12 </span>Is Elicited Divergence a Learning Signal?</h3>

<div id="S3.SS12.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS12.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our reward rests on the premise that questions eliciting larger teacher–student divergence along student trajectories provide more useful supervision. On natural questions, the support-aware score closely tracks its divergence component (Spearman <math id="S3.SS12.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho(\bar{s},\bar{\delta})=0.90" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>s</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>δ</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0.90</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho(\bar{s},\bar{\delta})=0.90</annotation></semantics></math>). We examine whether these signals predict actual learning gains through two experiments on the Qwen3-1.7B <math id="S3.SS12.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\leftarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\leftarrow</annotation></semantics></math> Qwen3-4B-RL-Math pair.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS12.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Per-question learning value.</h4>

<div id="S3.SS12.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS12.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For each of 1,500 held-out DAPO questions, we apply a single OPD update starting from the same base student and measure the resulting decrease in teacher–student KL on a fixed probe set. As shown in Figure <a href="#S3.F9" title="Figure 9 ‣ Downstream training utility. ‣ 3.12 Is Elicited Divergence a Learning Signal? ‣ 3 Experiments ‣ Ask to Distill: Learning to Synthesize Data for On-Policy Distillation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a> (left), mean divergence <math id="S3.SS12.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{\delta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>δ</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{\delta}</annotation></semantics></math> strongly correlates with this learning value (<math id="S3.SS12.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho=0.727" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.727</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho=0.727</annotation></semantics></math>, 95% CI <math id="S3.SS12.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[0.695,0.755]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0.695</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0.755</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[0.695,0.755]</annotation></semantics></math>). The association remains positive after controlling for response length, student pass rate, and student entropy (partial <math id="S3.SS12.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho=0.386" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.386</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho=0.386</annotation></semantics></math>, 95% CI <math id="S3.SS12.SSS0.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[0.329,0.443]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0.329</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0.443</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[0.329,0.443]</annotation></semantics></math>). The full score <math id="S3.SS12.SSS0.Px1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>s</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{s}</annotation></semantics></math> exhibits similar associations, with raw and partial correlations of <math id="S3.SS12.SSS0.Px1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.750" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.750</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.750</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS12.SSS0.Px1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.399" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.399</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.399</annotation></semantics></math>, respectively. Consistently, probe KL decrease rises monotonically across quintiles ranked by <math id="S3.SS12.SSS0.Px1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>s</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{s}</annotation></semantics></math>, with the highest quintile yielding <math id="S3.SS12.SSS0.Px1.p1.m10" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="2.15\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2.15</mn><mo lspace="0.222em">×</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2.15\times</annotation></semantics></math> the decrease of the lowest (middle).</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS12.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Downstream training utility.</h4>

<div id="S3.SS12.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS12.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We further train students on five score-ranked question banks under an identical token budget of 24.4M tokens, using three seeds per bank. Figure <a href="#S3.F9" title="Figure 9 ‣ Downstream training utility. ‣ 3.12 Is Elicited Divergence a Learning Signal? ‣ 3 Experiments ‣ Ask to Distill: Learning to Synthesize Data for On-Policy Distillation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a> (right) shows that higher-scoring banks generally yield better final accuracy across six benchmarks. Average accuracy rises from <math id="S3.SS12.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="41.06\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>41.06</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">41.06\%</annotation></semantics></math> in R1 to <math id="S3.SS12.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="43.49\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>43.49</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">43.49\%</annotation></semantics></math> in R4, while R5 reaches <math id="S3.SS12.SSS0.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="43.11\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>43.11</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">43.11\%</annotation></semantics></math>, exceeding R1 by <math id="S3.SS12.SSS0.Px2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2.05" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2.05</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2.05</annotation></semantics></math> percentage points. Thus, the overall trend favors higher-scoring banks, although performance is not strictly monotonic. Additional high-versus-low divergence comparisons retain a positive advantage after balancing compatibility (Appendix <a href="#A1.SS5.SSS0.Px8" title="Additional controlled comparisons. ‣ A.5 Experimental Details and Additional Signal Validation ‣ Appendix A Experimental Details ‣ Ask to Distill: Learning to Synthesize Data for On-Policy Distillation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.5</span></a>). Together, these results support elicited divergence as a useful indicator of distillation value, with evidence from both single-update gains and downstream training.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS12.SSS0.Px2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS12.SSS0.Px2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Both experiments use natural DAPO-17k-Math questions, without synthesizer outputs, to evaluate whether the proposed signals predict learning value independently of synthesizer optimization. The one-step analysis measures changes on a fixed, disjoint probe set, while downstream comparisons use a common training-token budget. Appendix <a href="#A1.SS5" title="A.5 Experimental Details and Additional Signal Validation ‣ Appendix A Experimental Details ‣ Ask to Distill: Learning to Synthesize Data for On-Policy Distillation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.5</span></a> provides the data splits, update and evaluation protocols, controlled designs, and statistical procedures.</p>
</div>
