<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Related Work</h2>

<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1 </span>On-Policy Distillation and Selective Supervision</h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">On-policy distillation supervises student-generated trajectories using teacher predictions at the same visited states. GKD addresses the distribution mismatch of fixed-response distillation by training on student-generated sequences <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Agarwal et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, while MiniLLM develops an on-policy optimization approach based on reverse KL divergence <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gu et al., 2024</a>)</cite>. Subsequent work improves the trajectories and teacher targets used for distillation. Speculative Knowledge Distillation interleaves student and teacher sampling to improve training sequences while accounting for the student’s generation distribution <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xu et al., 2025</a>)</cite>. Best-of-N Teacher Rollout Selection augments student-context supervision with an auxiliary teacher trajectory selected according to correctness and student alignment <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2026</a>)</cite>. PromptKD instead tunes teacher-side prompts to produce student-friendly supervision <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kim et al., 2024</a>)</cite>. These approaches modify how supervision is produced or delivered, whereas our method learns the questions that elicit it.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Selective OPD further recognizes that response positions provide unequal learning value. TIP characterizes token importance through student entropy and teacher–student divergence <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xu et al., 2026</a>)</cite>. Entropy-Aware OPD augments reverse KL with forward KL at high-teacher-entropy positions <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jin et al., 2026</a>)</cite>, and FiRe-OPD combines trajectory filtering with soft token reweighting <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2026c</a>)</cite>. Token Teachability distinguishes locally learnable disagreement from mismatch outside the student’s candidate support, using teacher–student discrepancy and teacher probability mass on the student’s top-<math id="S4.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> candidates <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2026b</a>)</cite>. In the related setting of on-policy self-distillation, matching supervision to student capacity has also been studied through joint token weighting and privileged-information adjustment <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yang et al., 2026b</a>)</cite>. We build on the support-aware characterization of teachability, adapting it into a question-level reward with trajectory aggregation, length control, and batch-diversity control. Our optimization changes the synthetic prompt distribution while holding the downstream OPD procedure fixed.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.2 </span>Synthetic Data Generation and Selection for Distillation</h3>

<div id="S4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Synthetic-data methods differ in the feedback used to construct training examples. Self-Instruct generates instruction–response pairs for supervised finetuning <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2023</a>)</cite>, and WizardLM evolves instructions toward greater complexity <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xu et al., 2024</a>)</cite>. R-Zero and Self-Questioning Language Models train task proposers through solver feedback to support self-improving reasoning <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Huang et al., 2026</a>; <a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al., 2025</a>)</cite>. Self-Adapting Language Models learns to generate finetuning data and update directives using the adapted model’s downstream performance <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zweiger et al., 2025</a>)</cite>. Particularly relevant to our setting, Prompting to Distill uses reinforced prompts and teacher–student discrepancy to synthesize data for text-classification distillation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ma et al., 2022</a>)</cite>. Our work extends the broader idea of feedback-guided synthesis to prompt-only OPD data, where value is assessed through teacher distributions along student-generated reasoning trajectories.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Recent studies directly examine the role of training questions in OPD. Rethinking OPD identifies teacher–student compatibility as an important factor and proposes teacher-aligned prompt selection <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2026b</a>)</cite>. Its follow-up shows that a single query can expose broad state coverage and recover much of the gain from full-data training <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Fu et al., 2026</a>)</cite>. What Matters in OPD studies difficulty-based selection, finding that a small set of hard questions can match a much larger dataset in its evaluated settings and relating their utility to longer reasoning trajectories <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hou et al., 2026</a>)</cite>. Beyond Prompt Count shows that prompt usefulness depends on the student–teacher pair and target capability, with targeted selection not consistently outperforming random sampling <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2026a</a>)</cite>. Data-Free OPD further demonstrates effective distillation using self-generated questions without external seed examples <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2026a</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">A2D</span> learns a synthesizer to actively shape the question distribution for a target student–teacher pair. Its objective evaluates the support-aware supervision elicited by each question, rather than using surface complexity, answer correctness, or raw disagreement alone as the generation criterion. Unlike selection from an existing question bank or direct sampling from an unoptimized model, our pipeline trains the synthesizer using a frozen scoring pair and then constructs a curated prompt-only bank for OPD. Question generation and synthesizer training reuse the target student and teacher without external training questions or auxiliary models. A frozen embedding model is used only to remove semantically redundant questions during post-generation curation.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5 </span>Conclusion</h2>

<div id="S5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We introduced <span id="S5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">A2D</span>, a framework that treats synthetic questions as distribution elicitors for on-policy distillation. Rather than judging a question by its surface form, <span id="S5.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">A2D</span> trains a student-initialized synthesizer against a frozen student–teacher pair to expose useful teacher supervision along student-generated trajectories. Its learning-value reward combines teacher–student disagreement with teacher probability mass on the student’s candidate support, together with length and diversity controls, and the generated questions are filtered and deduplicated into a prompt-only bank for standard OPD. Synthesizer training reuses the target student and teacher without external training questions or auxiliary models; a frozen embedding model is used only for post-generation semantic deduplication. Across mathematical reasoning, code generation, and agentic tasks, <span id="S5.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">A2D</span> improves average performance and teacher-gap recovery over unoptimized student-generated prompts and KL-filtered real questions, while matching or exceeding real-bank OPD in the evaluated settings. Signal analyses further show that optimized questions can elicit denser supervision from shorter student responses. These results support elicited distributional supervision as an objective for constructing synthetic distillation data.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Our learning-value reward is a surrogate estimated from a single student response per question, and the synthesizer is optimized once for a fixed student–teacher pair. Developing more effective adaptive question-generation strategies as the student improves, and combining learned question generation with token-level selection methods for OPD, are natural directions for future work.</p>
</div>
</section>
