<section id="A1.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">A.2 </span>Implementation Details</h3>

<div id="A1.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our framework was developed based on verl <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib45" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sheng et al., 2025</a>)</cite>. For reward computation, we use <math id="A1.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K=16" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>16</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K=16</annotation></semantics></math> candidate tokens, a question-generation budget of <math id="A1.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{q}=4096" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>4096</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{q}=4096</annotation></semantics></math> tokens, and a student-response budget of <math id="A1.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{\max}=7168" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>max</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>7168</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{\max}=7168</annotation></semantics></math> tokens, with the latter matched to the downstream OPD response budget by construction (§<a href="#S2.SS6" title="2.6 On-policy distillation ‣ 2 Method ‣ Ask to Distill: Learning to Synthesize Data for On-Policy Distillation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.6</span></a>). For each student–teacher pair, the discrepancy scale <math id="A1.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{\mathrm{ref}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>ref</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{\mathrm{ref}}</annotation></semantics></math> is set to the <math id="A1.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa=95" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>95</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa=95</annotation></semantics></math>th percentile of <math id="A1.SS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> estimated from <math id="A1.SS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{\mathrm{cal}}=200" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>cal</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>200</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{\mathrm{cal}}=200</annotation></semantics></math> questions, and we use <math id="A1.SS2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon=10^{-8}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon=10^{-8}</annotation></semantics></math> in Eq. (<a href="#S2.E6" title="Equation 6 ‣ Gating discrepancy by compatibility. ‣ 2.3 The learning-value reward ‣ 2 Method ‣ Ask to Distill: Learning to Synthesize Data for On-Policy Distillation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>). For reward shaping, we set the aggregation exponent <math id="A1.SS2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha=0.5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha=0.5</annotation></semantics></math>, the length-penalty coefficient <math id="A1.SS2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{\mathrm{len}}=0.3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>len</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0.3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{\mathrm{len}}=0.3</annotation></semantics></math>, the diversity-penalty coefficient <math id="A1.SS2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{\mathrm{div}}=1.0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>div</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1.0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{\mathrm{div}}=1.0</annotation></semantics></math>, and the clustering threshold <math id="A1.SS2.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau=0.1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau=0.1</annotation></semantics></math>. The synthetic-data synthesizer is optimized with GRPO using 8 prompts and a group size of <math id="A1.SS2.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g=8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g=8</annotation></semantics></math>, yielding 64 candidate questions per step. We use a learning rate of <math id="A1.SS2.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\times 10^{-6}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\times 10^{-6}</annotation></semantics></math>, entropy and KL coefficients of 0.01 and 0.005, respectively, and a rollout temperature of 1.0, training for 60 steps and saving checkpoints every 5 steps. We generate <math id="A1.SS2.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N=20{,}000" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>20,000</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N=20{,}000</annotation></semantics></math> raw questions in 10 shards of 2000 samples each and apply Jaccard and cosine deduplication thresholds of <math id="A1.SS2.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{\mathrm{lex}}=0.7" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>lex</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0.7</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{\mathrm{lex}}=0.7</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.SS2.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{\mathrm{sem}}=0.95" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>sem</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0.95</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{\mathrm{sem}}=0.95</annotation></semantics></math>, respectively. Downstream OPD uses token-level rewards directly as advantages, with the student’s top-<math id="A1.SS2.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> candidates (<math id="A1.SS2.p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K=16" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>16</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K=16</annotation></semantics></math>) weighted by <math id="A1.SS2.p1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{p}_{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>p</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>S</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{p}_{S}</annotation></semantics></math>. We sample <math id="A1.SS2.p1.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{\mathrm{roll}}=4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>roll</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{\mathrm{roll}}=4</annotation></semantics></math> responses per prompt at temperature 1.0 and train with a batch size of 64, a learning rate of <math id="A1.SS2.p1.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\times 10^{-6}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\times 10^{-6}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <span id="A1.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">fp32</span> parameters for 2 epochs, saving checkpoints every 5 steps. For each student–teacher pair and each OPD method, we select a single downstream student checkpoint based on AMC23 performance for mathematical reasoning and LiveCodeBench performance for code generation. All benchmark scores reported for that method are evaluated at this same checkpoint, rather than selected independently for each benchmark. AMC23 and LiveCodeBench therefore serve as checkpoint-selection benchmarks; their reported scores are not held-out estimates.</p>
</div>
</section>
