<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">A.3 </span>Evaluation Details</h3>

<section id="A1.SS3.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Metric.</h4>

<div id="A1.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For each benchmark, we sample <math id="A1.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> responses per problem and report avg@<math id="A1.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>: the fraction of correct responses for each problem, averaged over problems and scaled to <math id="A1.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[0,100]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0,100</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[0,100]</annotation></semantics></math>. With <math id="A1.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=1</annotation></semantics></math>, this reduces to single-sample accuracy. All reported results are means over three independent runs with different random seeds. The reported averages weight each benchmark equally, regardless of its number of problems. Table <a href="#A1.T5" title="Table 5 ‣ Code generation. ‣ A.3 Evaluation Details ‣ Appendix A Experimental Details ‣ Ask to Distill: Learning to Synthesize Data for On-Policy Distillation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> lists the per-benchmark settings. All evaluations use vLLM (v0.11.0) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kwon et al., 2023</a>)</cite> with bf16 inference on NVIDIA A100 80GB GPUs.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A1.SS3.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Mathematical reasoning.</h4>

<div id="A1.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We use the DAPO prompt format, which asks the model to reason step by step and to give its final answer within <span id="A1.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">\boxed{}</span>. Qwen3 students and teachers are evaluated in non-thinking mode, whereas DeepSeek-R1-Distill students and their teachers (JustRL and Skywork-OR1) use their default thinking templates. We extract the last <span id="A1.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">\boxed{}</span> expression, falling back to the last “Answer:” line, and verify it against the reference answer by rule-based string normalization and symbolic equivalence checking with SymPy <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib46" title="" class="ltx_ref">Meurer et al., 2017</a>)</cite>; no model-based judge is used.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A1.SS3.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Code generation.</h4>

<div id="A1.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For LiveCodeBench, we use the v5–v6 release subset of 342 problems published between September 2024 and April 2025, with the original LiveCodeBench prompt. For APPS, we use the full introductory and competition test splits (1,000 problems each) and 1,000 interview problems drawn from the test split with a fixed random seed, keeping at most 50 test cases per problem; prompts follow the LiveCodeBench standard-input template. MBPP+ uses EvalPlus v0.2.0 (378 problems) with its standard prompt template and greedy decoding. Qwen3 models are evaluated in non-thinking mode. We extract the last fenced code block from each response and execute it against all test cases; a response is counted as correct only if it passes every test within the time limit. In the math-to-code transfer experiment (Figure <a href="#S3.F7" title="Figure 7 ‣ Math-to-code transfer. ‣ 3.8 Cross-Domain Transfer between Math and Code ‣ 3 Experiments ‣ Ask to Distill: Learning to Synthesize Data for On-Policy Distillation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>), the Qwen3 models use the non-thinking settings in Table <a href="#A1.T5" title="Table 5 ‣ Code generation. ‣ A.3 Evaluation Details ‣ Appendix A Experimental Details ‣ Ask to Distill: Learning to Synthesize Data for On-Policy Distillation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>, whereas DeepSeek-R1-Distill students and their mathematics teachers are evaluated in thinking mode with their default chat templates, using temperature 0.6, top-<math id="A1.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> 0.95, top-<math id="A1.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> 20, and a maximum generation length of 32,768 tokens on all three benchmarks.</p>
</div>
