Accuracy gains of high- over low-divergence training groups.
Each cell reports the gain in percentage points,
its 95% confidence interval, and <math id="A1.F10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math>-value.
</span></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A1.SS5" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">A.5 </span>Experimental Details and Additional Signal Validation</h3>

<section id="A1.SS5.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Data and splits.</h4>

<div id="A1.SS5.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS5.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">All questions in these analyses are natural questions from DAPO-17k-Math; no synthesizer outputs are used. Among 17,177 eligible questions, we exclude a 128-question probe set and 500 questions reserved for token-level analysis from downstream training, leaving a pool of 16,549 questions. The one-step analysis uses 1,500 update questions disjoint from the probe set: the 500 token-level questions and 1,000 additional questions from the training pool. The one-step and downstream training analyses are conducted independently.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A1.SS5.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">One-step learning value.</h4>

<div id="A1.SS5.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS5.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For each update question, we apply a single plain-SGD OPD step with a learning rate of <math id="A1.SS5.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3\times 10^{-5}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3\times 10^{-5}</annotation></semantics></math> using four responses sampled from the base student. Each question is evaluated starting from the same base-student parameters. Its learning value is the resulting decrease in full-vocabulary <math id="A1.SS5.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathrm{KL}(\pi_{T}\|\pi_{S})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>KL</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{KL}(\pi_{T}\|\pi_{S})</annotation></semantics></math> on the fixed probe set, averaged over tokens of one fixed base-student response per probe question. This measurement differs from the OPD update in its questions, prefixes, and KL direction: the update uses a reverse-KL-style objective on the student’s top-16 candidate tokens, whereas the probe evaluates full-vocabulary teacher-to-student KL on separate questions and fixed prefixes.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A1.SS5.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Test–retest reliability.</h4>

<div id="A1.SS5.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS5.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We estimate test–retest reliability on 200 of the update questions by repeating the analysis with an independent draw of four student responses per question. For each pair of quantities being correlated, we compute a reliability-corrected correlation by dividing the observed Spearman correlation by the square root of the product of their two test–retest reliabilities. We report this correction as a supplementary analysis of sensitivity to rollout-sampling noise.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A1.SS5.SSS0.Px4" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Score-ranked training banks.</h4>

<div id="A1.SS5.SSS0.Px4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS5.SSS0.Px4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We partition the training pool into five approximately equal-sized buckets by mean support-aware score <math id="A1.SS5.SSS0.Px4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>s</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{s}</annotation></semantics></math>, yielding approximately 3,310 questions per bucket. A random bank of the same size serves as a reference. Each bank is evaluated using three training seeds.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A1.SS5.SSS0.Px5" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Factorial designs and divergence matching.</h4>

<div id="A1.SS5.SSS0.Px5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS5.SSS0.Px5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For the <math id="A1.SS5.SSS0.Px5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2\times 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2\times 2</annotation></semantics></math> designs, we select the bottom and top tertiles of mean divergence <math id="A1.SS5.SSS0.Px5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{\delta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>δ</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{\delta}</annotation></semantics></math>, corresponding to <math id="A1.SS5.SSS0.Px5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{\delta}\leq 0.290" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>δ</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo>≤</mo><mn>0.290</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{\delta}\leq 0.290</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.SS5.SSS0.Px5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{\delta}\geq 0.404" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>δ</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo>≥</mo><mn>0.404</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{\delta}\geq 0.404</annotation></semantics></math>, respectively. Within each tertile, we split questions at the median of mean compatibility <math id="A1.SS5.SSS0.Px5.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{\omega}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>ω</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{\omega}</annotation></semantics></math>, producing four cells of approximately 2,758 questions each. Because <math id="A1.SS5.SSS0.Px5.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{\delta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>δ</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{\delta}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.SS5.SSS0.Px5.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{\omega}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>ω</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{\omega}</annotation></semantics></math> are negatively correlated, this split can leave differences in divergence between the compatibility cells. The <math id="A1.SS5.SSS0.Px5.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta</annotation></semantics></math>-matched variant therefore divides each divergence tertile into 20 strata by <math id="A1.SS5.SSS0.Px5.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{\delta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>δ</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{\delta}</annotation></semantics></math> and splits <math id="A1.SS5.SSS0.Px5.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{\omega}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>ω</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{\omega}</annotation></semantics></math> at its median within each stratum. This procedure approximately balances mean divergence between the two compatibility cells within each divergence level: 0.528 versus 0.531 in the high-divergence tertile and 0.229 versus 0.230 in the low-divergence tertile. Each resulting cell contains approximately 2,758 questions.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A1.SS5.SSS0.Px6" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Training budgets and checkpoints.</h4>

<div id="A1.SS5.SSS0.Px6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS5.SSS0.Px6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">All banks use a common target budget of 24.37M training tokens. Runs reach this budget within 26–36 steps, including 30–31 steps for the five score buckets and the random reference bank, and consume at most 2,304 questions. No question is used more than once within a run. Seeds differ in question order and therefore in the subset consumed before reaching the budget. We evaluate the end checkpoint at the first step reaching the full budget. For the mid checkpoint, we use the first saved checkpoint reaching half the budget, with checkpoints saved every five steps. Consequently, mid checkpoints span 12.4M–18.2M tokens across banks and provide less tightly budget-controlled comparisons than end checkpoints.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A1.SS5.SSS0.Px7" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Statistical analysis.</h4>

<div id="A1.SS5.SSS0.Px7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS5.SSS0.Px7.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Confidence intervals for the one-step analysis are obtained by bootstrapping over the 1,500 update questions. For downstream training, benchmark scores are first averaged within each run, and the training run is the unit of inference, with three seeds per bank. Pairwise contrasts use run-level two-sample <math id="A1.SS5.SSS0.Px7.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>-tests, and effects in the <math id="A1.SS5.SSS0.Px7.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2\times 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2\times 2</annotation></semantics></math> designs are evaluated using a two-way ANOVA over runs. The Jonckheere–Terpstra test assesses the ordered trend across the five score buckets using permutations over their 15 training runs. Figure 9 reports means and standard deviations across seeds.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A1.SS5.SSS0.Px8" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Additional controlled comparisons.</h4>

<div id="A1.SS5.SSS0.Px8.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS5.SSS0.Px8.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To examine whether the training advantage associated with higher divergence persists after accounting for compatibility, we compare high- and low-divergence question banks under both the original design and a design balanced on the compatibility factor <math id="A1.SS5.SSS0.Px8.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math>. Figure <a href="#A1.F10" title="Figure 10 ‣ Combining with filtering and OPD variants ‣ A.4 Discussion ‣ Appendix A Experimental Details ‣ Ask to Distill: Learning to Synthesize Data for On-Policy Distillation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a> reports accuracy differences in percentage points at intermediate and final training checkpoints, together with 95% confidence intervals and <math id="A1.SS5.SSS0.Px8.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math>-values.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS5.SSS0.Px8.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS5.SSS0.Px8.p2.1" class="ltx_p">At the final checkpoint, high-divergence banks outperform low-divergence banks by <math id="A1.SS5.SSS0.Px8.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2.21" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2.21</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2.21</annotation></semantics></math> points in the original design (95% CI <math id="A1.SS5.SSS0.Px8.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[1.37,3.06]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>1.37</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3.06</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[1.37,3.06]</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A1.SS5.SSS0.Px8.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p=3.1\times 10^{-4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>3.1</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p=3.1\times 10^{-4}</annotation></semantics></math>). After balancing <math id="A1.SS5.SSS0.Px8.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math>, the advantage remains positive at <math id="A1.SS5.SSS0.Px8.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1.59" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1.59</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1.59</annotation></semantics></math> points (95% CI <math id="A1.SS5.SSS0.Px8.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[0.63,2.56]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0.63</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2.56</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[0.63,2.56]</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A1.SS5.SSS0.Px8.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p=0.005" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.005</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p=0.005</annotation></semantics></math>). The intermediate checkpoint shows the same direction: gains of <math id="A1.SS5.SSS0.Px8.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2.27" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2.27</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2.27</annotation></semantics></math> points in the original design (95% CI <math id="A1.SS5.SSS0.Px8.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[0.62,3.91]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0.62</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3.91</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[0.62,3.91]</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A1.SS5.SSS0.Px8.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p=0.013" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.013</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p=0.013</annotation></semantics></math>) and <math id="A1.SS5.SSS0.Px8.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2.79" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2.79</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2.79</annotation></semantics></math> points after balancing <math id="A1.SS5.SSS0.Px8.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math> (95% CI <math id="A1.SS5.SSS0.Px8.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[1.75,3.82]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>1.75</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3.82</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[1.75,3.82]</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A1.SS5.SSS0.Px8.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p=2.6\times 10^{-4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>2.6</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p=2.6\times 10^{-4}</annotation></semantics></math>). All four confidence intervals exclude zero, supporting a positive training advantage for high-divergence banks that persists under compatibility balancing.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A2" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix B </span>Theoretical Properties of the Elicited-Signal Objective</h2>

<div id="A2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We establish two properties of our framework: high normalized
reward certifies positions with simultaneous discrepancy and
compatibility, and compatibility enters the implemented OPD
advantages through an explicit log-ratio term. These results
characterize the elicited supervision and its connection to
the update, without assuming convergence of synthesizer
optimization.</p>
</div>
<section id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Notation.</h4>

<div id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Fix the scoring student <math id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{S}</annotation></semantics></math> and teacher <math id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{T}</annotation></semantics></math>.
For a question <math id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math>, let <math id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="y\sim\pi_{S}(\cdot\mid q)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y\sim\pi_{S}(\cdot\mid q)</annotation></semantics></math> be a
nonempty response of length <math id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\leq L\leq L_{\max}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>max</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\leq L\leq L_{\max}</annotation></semantics></math>.
At position <math id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>, let <math id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{S,t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{S,t}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{T,t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{T,t}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the
next-token distributions on the student-generated prefix,
and define</p>
<table id="A2.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{t}=\mathrm{Top}\text{-}K(p_{S,t}),\qquad B_{t}=\mathrm{Top}\text{-}K(p_{T,t}),\qquad U_{t}=A_{t}\cup B_{t}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>Top</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>-</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>Top</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>-</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∪</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{t}=\mathrm{Top}\text{-}K(p_{S,t}),\qquad B_{t}=\mathrm{Top}\text{-}K(p_{T,t}),\qquad U_{t}=A_{t}\cup B_{t}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">For a nonempty token set <math id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math>, write
<math id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{M,t}(C)=\sum_{v\in C}p_{M,t}(v)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{M,t}(C)=\sum_{v\in C}p_{M,t}(v)</annotation></semantics></math> and let
<math id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{p}_{M,t}^{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>p</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mi>C</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{p}_{M,t}^{C}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the distribution conditioned
on <math id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math>, extended by zero outside <math id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math>.
We assume strictly positive next-token probabilities.
All logarithms are natural.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Following Eqs. (<a href="#S2.E4" title="Equation 4 ‣ Per-position quantities. ‣ 2.3 The learning-value reward ‣ 2 Method ‣ Ask to Distill: Learning to Synthesize Data for On-Policy Distillation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>)–(<a href="#S2.E6" title="Equation 6 ‣ Gating discrepancy by compatibility. ‣ 2.3 The learning-value reward ‣ 2 Method ‣ Ask to Distill: Learning to Synthesize Data for On-Policy Distillation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>), write</p>
<table id="A2.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{t}=\mathrm{KL}\!\left(\bar{p}_{T,t}^{U_{t}}\,\middle\|\,\bar{p}_{S,t}^{U_{t}}\right),\qquad\omega_{t}=p_{T,t}(A_{t})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mpadded style="width:1.431em;" width="1.431em"><mi>KL</mi></mpadded><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>p</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></msubsup><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0.170em" stretchy="true">∥</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>p</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></msubsup><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{t}=\mathrm{KL}\!\left(\bar{p}_{T,t}^{U_{t}}\,\middle\|\,\bar{p}_{S,t}^{U_{t}}\right),\qquad\omega_{t}=p_{T,t}(A_{t}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">and</p>
<table id="A2.Ex3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{t}=\min\{\delta_{t}/\Delta,1\}\omega_{t},\qquad\Delta=\delta_{\mathrm{ref}}+\epsilon&gt;0." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>ref</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{t}=\min\{\delta_{t}/\Delta,1\}\omega_{t},\qquad\Delta=\delta_{\mathrm{ref}}+\epsilon&gt;0.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.3" class="ltx_p">Thus <math id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{t}\in[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{t}\in[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math>. With
<math id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{s}=L^{-1}\sum_{t=1}^{L}s_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>s</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>L</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>L</mi></msubsup><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{s}=L^{-1}\sum_{t=1}^{L}s_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>, the reward in
Eq. (<a href="#S2.E11" title="Equation 11 ‣ Final question-level reward. ‣ 2.3 The learning-value reward ‣ 2 Method ‣ Ask to Distill: Learning to Synthesize Data for On-Policy Distillation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11</span></a>) is</p>
<table id="A2.Ex4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R=P_{\mathrm{len}}P_{\mathrm{div}}L^{1-\alpha}\bar{s},\qquad 0&lt;\alpha&lt;1," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>len</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>div</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>L</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>s</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>α</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R=P_{\mathrm{len}}P_{\mathrm{div}}L^{1-\alpha}\bar{s},\qquad 0&lt;\alpha&lt;1,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.4" class="ltx_p">where both penalty factors lie in <math id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math>.
We suppress the dependence of <math id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math> on the sampled response
and scoring batch, and write <math id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[x]_{+}=\max\{x,0\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[x]_{+}=\max\{x,0\}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">B.1 </span>Compatible-disagreement coverage</h3>

<div id="A2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For a threshold <math id="A2.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h\in(0,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h\in(0,1)</annotation></semantics></math>, define</p>
<table id="A2.Ex5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathcal{E}_{h}=\{t:\delta_{t}\geq h\Delta,\ \omega_{t}\geq h\}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>≥</mo><mi>h</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>≥</mo><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{E}_{h}=\{t:\delta_{t}\geq h\Delta,\ \omega_{t}\geq h\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">These positions simultaneously exhibit teacher–student
discrepancy and teacher probability mass within the
student’s candidate set.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proposition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="A2.Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition B.1</span></span><span id="A2.Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Compatible-disagreement coverage)<span id="A2.Thmtheorem1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="A2.Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For every nonempty response,</span></p>
<table id="A2.Ex6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{|\mathcal{E}_{h}|}{L}\geq\left[\frac{\bar{s}-h}{1-h}\right]_{+}\geq\left[\frac{R/L^{1-\alpha}-h}{1-h}\right]_{+}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mi>L</mi></mfrac><mo>≥</mo><msub><mrow><mo>[</mo><mfrac><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>s</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo>−</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo></msub><mo>≥</mo><msub><mrow><mo>[</mo><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>/</mo><msup><mi>L</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{|\mathcal{E}_{h}|}{L}\geq\left[\frac{\bar{s}-h}{1-h}\right]_{+}\geq\left[\frac{R/L^{1-\alpha}-h}{1-h}\right]_{+}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS1.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="A2.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text">If <math id="A2.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\notin\mathcal{E}_{h}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∉</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mi>h</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\notin\mathcal{E}_{h}</annotation></semantics></math>, then either
<math id="A2.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{t}&lt;h\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{t}&lt;h\Delta</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="A2.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega_{t}&lt;h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega_{t}&lt;h</annotation></semantics></math>, and hence <math id="A2.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{t}\leq h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>≤</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{t}\leq h</annotation></semantics></math>.
Since <math id="A2.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{t}\leq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>≤</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{t}\leq 1</annotation></semantics></math> at every position,</span></p>
<table id="A2.Ex7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{s}\leq h+(1-h)\frac{|\mathcal{E}_{h}|}{L}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>s</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mi>L</mi></mfrac></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{s}\leq h+(1-h)\frac{|\mathcal{E}_{h}|}{L}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS1.p2.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS1.p2.2.1" class="ltx_text">Rearranging, using nonnegativity, and noting that</span></p>
<table id="A2.Ex8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R/L^{1-\alpha}=P_{\mathrm{len}}P_{\mathrm{div}}\bar{s}\leq\bar{s}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>/</mo><msup><mi>L</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>len</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>div</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>s</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>s</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R/L^{1-\alpha}=P_{\mathrm{len}}P_{\mathrm{div}}\bar{s}\leq\bar{s}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS1.p2.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS1.p2.3.1" class="ltx_text">gives the result.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Thus, a normalized reward above <math id="A2.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math> certifies a nonzero
fraction of positions exhibiting both discrepancy and
compatibility. Normalization by <math id="A2.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L^{1-\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>L</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L^{1-\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math> is
necessary because the unnormalized reward also depends
on response length.</p>
</div>
<section id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Expected coverage.</h4>

<div id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Sample a scoring batch, select a question uniformly,
sample its student response, and select a response
position uniformly. Then</p>
<table id="A2.Ex9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pr(t\in\mathcal{E}_{h})\geq\left[\frac{\mathbb{E}[R/L^{1-\alpha}]-h}{1-h}\right]_{+}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>Pr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mi>h</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><msub><mrow><mo>[</mo><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>/</mo><msup><mi>L</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pr(t\in\mathcal{E}_{h})\geq\left[\frac{\mathbb{E}[R/L^{1-\alpha}]-h}{1-h}\right]_{+}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">This follows by taking expectations of the pointwise
inequality in the proof above and using nonnegativity.
The expectation includes question generation, student
responses, and the batch-dependent diversity factor.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">B.2 </span>Compatibility and OPD advantages</h3>

<div id="A2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We next relate compatibility to the candidate advantages
used in §<a href="#S2.SS6" title="2.6 On-policy distillation ‣ 2 Method ‣ Ask to Distill: Learning to Synthesize Data for On-Policy Distillation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.6</span></a>. Unlike the coverage certificate
computed with the frozen scoring student, the following
identity holds for any rollout policy during OPD.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.Thmtheorem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proposition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="A2.Thmtheorem2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition B.2</span></span><span id="A2.Thmtheorem2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Compatibility in the OPD advantage)<span id="A2.Thmtheorem2.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="A2.Thmtheorem2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.Thmtheorem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.Thmtheorem2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Fix a response position and let
<math id="A2.Thmtheorem2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{S}=p_{S,\mathrm{old}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>old</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{S}=p_{S,\mathrm{old}}</annotation></semantics></math> be the rollout-policy
distribution. Write</span></p>
<table id="A2.Ex10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A=\mathrm{Top}\text{-}K(p_{S}),\qquad a=p_{S}(A),\qquad\omega=p_{T}(A)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>Top</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">-</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><mi>ω</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A=\mathrm{Top}\text{-}K(p_{S}),\qquad a=p_{S}(A),\qquad\omega=p_{T}(A).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.Thmtheorem2.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.Thmtheorem2.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For the candidate advantages in
Eq. (<a href="#S2.E13" title="Equation 13 ‣ 2.6 On-policy distillation ‣ 2 Method ‣ Ask to Distill: Learning to Synthesize Data for On-Policy Distillation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">13</span></a>),</span></p>
<table id="A2.Ex11" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r(v)=\bar{p}_{S}^{A}(v)\log\frac{p_{T}(v)}{p_{S}(v)},\qquad v\in A," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>p</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>S</mi><mi>A</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r(v)=\bar{p}_{S}^{A}(v)\log\frac{p_{T}(v)}{p_{S}(v)},\qquad v\in A,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.Thmtheorem2.p1.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.Thmtheorem2.p1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">we have</span></p>
<table id="A2.Ex12" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{v\in A}r(v)=-\mathrm{KL}\!\left(\bar{p}_{S}^{A}\,\middle\|\,\bar{p}_{T}^{A}\right)+\log\frac{\omega}{a}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mpadded style="width:1.431em;" width="1.431em"><mi>KL</mi></mpadded><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>p</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>S</mi><mi>A</mi></msubsup><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0.170em" stretchy="true">∥</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>p</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>T</mi><mi>A</mi></msubsup><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mfrac><mi>ω</mi><mi>a</mi></mfrac></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{v\in A}r(v)=-\mathrm{KL}\!\left(\bar{p}_{S}^{A}\,\middle\|\,\bar{p}_{T}^{A}\right)+\log\frac{\omega}{a}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.Thmtheorem2.p1.4" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.Thmtheorem2.p1.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">If the position is scored using these same student and
teacher distributions, then <math id="A2.Thmtheorem2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s\geq h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>≥</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s\geq h</annotation></semantics></math> implies
<math id="A2.Thmtheorem2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\log(\omega/a)\geq\log h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ω</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\log(\omega/a)\geq\log h</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS2.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="A2.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text">For each <math id="A2.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v\in A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v\in A</annotation></semantics></math>,</span></p>
<table id="A2.Ex13" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\log\frac{p_{T}(v)}{p_{S}(v)}=\log\frac{\bar{p}_{T}^{A}(v)}{\bar{p}_{S}^{A}(v)}+\log\frac{\omega}{a}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mfrac><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>p</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>T</mi><mi>A</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>p</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>S</mi><mi>A</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mfrac><mi>ω</mi><mi>a</mi></mfrac></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\log\frac{p_{T}(v)}{p_{S}(v)}=\log\frac{\bar{p}_{T}^{A}(v)}{\bar{p}_{S}^{A}(v)}+\log\frac{\omega}{a}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS2.p2.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS2.p2.2.1" class="ltx_text">Multiplying by <math id="A2.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{p}_{S}^{A}(v)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>p</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>S</mi><mi>A</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{p}_{S}^{A}(v)</annotation></semantics></math> and summing over <math id="A2.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math>
gives the identity. The final claim follows from
<math id="A2.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega\geq s\geq h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ω</mi><mo>≥</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>≥</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega\geq s\geq h</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A2.SS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a\leq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a\leq 1</annotation></semantics></math>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Compatibility therefore contributes a common additive
offset to the candidate log-ratios, while the conditional
distributions determine their relative values.
When <math id="A2.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega&lt;a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ω</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega&lt;a</annotation></semantics></math>, this offset is negative; at
high-scoring positions, it is bounded below by <math id="A2.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\log h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\log h</annotation></semantics></math>.
The identity characterizes the fixed advantages,
rather than the resulting parameter-gradient norm.</p>
</div>
<section id="A2.SS2.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Teacher truncation.</h4>

<div id="A2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Compatibility also has a distributional interpretation:</p>
<table id="A2.Ex14" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{TV}(p_{T},\bar{p}_{T}^{A})=1-\omega," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>TV</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>p</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>T</mi><mi>A</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>ω</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{TV}(p_{T},\bar{p}_{T}^{A})=1-\omega,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="A2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{TV}(P,Q)=\frac{1}{2}\sum_{v}|P(v)-Q(v)|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>TV</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0em">∑</mo><mi>v</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{TV}(P,Q)=\frac{1}{2}\sum_{v}|P(v)-Q(v)|</annotation></semantics></math>.
Indeed, the total absolute difference inside <math id="A2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> is
<math id="A2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1-\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>ω</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1-\omega</annotation></semantics></math>, as is the teacher mass outside <math id="A2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math>.
Consequently, <math id="A2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{t}\geq h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>≥</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{t}\geq h</annotation></semantics></math> implies</p>
<table id="A2.Ex15" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{TV}(p_{T,t},\bar{p}_{T,t}^{A_{t}})\leq 1-h." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>TV</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>p</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{TV}(p_{T,t},\bar{p}_{T,t}^{A_{t}})\leq 1-h.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.3" class="ltx_p">Here <math id="A2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{p}_{T}^{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>p</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>T</mi><mi>A</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{p}_{T}^{A}</annotation></semantics></math> is an analysis device: the implemented
update uses full-vocabulary teacher log-probabilities.
Moreover, a fixed top-<math id="A2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> set need not contain most of
the student’s probability mass; this must be assessed
separately through <math id="A2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{S}(A)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{S}(A)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A2.SS2.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Scope.</h4>

<div id="A2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">These results characterize the scoring signal and its
relationship to the fixed OPD advantages. They do not
lower-bound parameter gradients or downstream
improvement: the scoring discrepancy is computed on
<math id="A2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>, whereas explicit candidate-level update terms
are restricted to <math id="A2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>, and the scoring and update
divergences have different directions. Coverage
certificates computed with the frozen scoring student
also need not persist as the student evolves during
OPD. Compatibility concerns candidate probability mass,
rather than question correctness or membership in
either model’s training distribution. The effectiveness
of the elicited supervision for student learning is
evaluated empirically in §<a href="#S3" title="3 Experiments ‣ Ask to Distill: Learning to Synthesize Data for On-Policy Distillation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>.</p>
</div>
