<span id="A3.p3.pic1.2.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.p3.pic1.2.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_sansserif" style="font-size:90%;--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">FIRST, in your private scratch-pad, think step-by-step to design a brand-new, non-trivial competitive programming problem on your own subject. Aim for a difficulty such that a strong contestant would need several non-obvious algorithmic insights, not a single library call. Avoid re-using classic textbook tasks or well-known contest problems.</span></span>
<span id="A3.p3.pic1.2.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.p3.pic1.2.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_sansserif" style="font-size:90%;--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">The problem must be self-contained and must state its Input format, its Output format, and the constraints on the input. The program that solves it reads from standard input and writes to standard output.</span></span>
